var SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture Overview", "subtitle": "System design, communication patterns, threading model, and how all the pieces fit together.", "headings": [{"id": "high-level", "text": "High-Level Architecture", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-frontend", "text": "Backend / Frontend Split", "level": 2}, {"id": "manager-pattern", "text": "The Manager Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "communication", "text": "Python-QML Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Context Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 3}, {"id": "properties", "text": "Qt Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "qml-types", "text": "Custom QML Types", "level": 3}, {"id": "startup", "text": "Application Startup Sequence", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "thread-obd", "text": "1. QThread Worker (OBD Connection)", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-pool", "text": "2. ThreadPoolExecutor (Spotify, Network, Download)", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-timer", "text": "3. QTimer Polling (Sensors, ESP32)", "level": 3}, {"id": "volume-system", "text": "Volume System", "level": 2}, {"id": "image-providers", "text": "Image Providers", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-detection", "text": "Platform Detection & Stubs", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-persistence", "text": "Settings Persistence", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging System", "level": 2}, {"id": "signal-wiring", "text": "Signal Wiring Graph", "level": 2}, {"id": "cleanup", "text": "Shutdown & Cleanup", "level": 2}], "body": "Architecture - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Architecture Architecture Overview System design, communication patterns, threading model, and how all the pieces fit together. High-Level Architecture OCTAVE follows a clean backend/frontend separation connected by Qt's signals and slots mechanism. OCTAVE maintains two parallel backends that expose the same API to the shared frontend: a C++ / Qt 6 backend ( src/ ) \u2014 the only shipped binary on every platform \u2014 and a Python / PySide6 backend ( backend/ ) used for desktop development and tinkering. Either one handles all business logic, hardware communication, API calls, and data processing; the QML frontend handles all rendering, animation, and user interaction, and works identically against either backend. \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 OCTAVE Application \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 Signals/Slots \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Backend (C++ / Py) \u2502 \u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba \u2502 QML Frontend \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Context Props \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 Main.qml \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 SettingsManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 MediaPlayer \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 MediaManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 OBDMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 SpotifyManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 CarMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 OBDManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Settings/* \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 NetworkManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 BottomBar \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DownloadManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 ... \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 ESP32VolumeMgr \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 BerryIMUManager \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 GestureManager \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 AndroidAutoMgr \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Image Providers \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 PhoneMirrorMgr \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 dhuframe (Android Auto) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 AudioAnalyzer \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 scrcpyframe (Phone Mirror) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Clock \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Qt 6 Runtime (C++ \u00b7 or PySide6) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 (QGuiApplication / QApplication) \u2192 QQmlEngine \u2192 Event Loop\u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 Backend / Frontend Split Layer Technology Location Responsibility Backend (shipped) C++ / Qt 6 src/ Business logic, hardware I/O, API calls, data processing, state management \u2014 the only binary shipped on every platform Backend (dev peer) Python 3.8+ / PySide6 backend/ API-equivalent backend for desktop development, prototyping, and Raspberry Pi tinkering Frontend QML (Qt Quick 2.15) frontend/ UI rendering, animations, user interaction, layout \u2014 identical against either backend Bridge C++ main / PySide6 src/main.cpp \u00b7 main.py Registers managers as context properties, wires signals, manages lifecycle The backend never touches the UI directly. The frontend never makes API calls or accesses hardware. All communication goes through Qt's signal/slot mechanism, which is thread-safe and decoupled. The Manager Pattern Every backend feature is encapsulated in a manager class that inherits from QObject . This is the fundamental pattern used throughout OCTAVE: from PySide6.QtCore import QObject, Signal, Slot, Property class ExampleManager (QObject): # Signals \u2014 emitted when state changes, QML listens to these valueChanged = Signal( int ) def __init__ (self): super ().__init__() self._value = 0 # Slots \u2014 callable from QML @Slot( int ) def setValue (self, v): self._value = v self.valueChanged.emit(v) # Properties \u2014 bindable from QML @Property( int , notify=valueChanged) def value (self): return self._value Key principles: Signals notify the UI when backend state changes Slots are Python methods callable from QML Properties expose readable (and optionally writable) values to QML with automatic binding Managers are self-contained \u2014 each owns its state and logic Cross-manager communication goes through signal wiring in main.py All Managers Manager File QML Context Property Purpose SettingsManager settings_manager.py settingsManager Central settings store Clock clock.py clock Real-time clock MediaManager media_manager.py mediaManager Local audio playback AudioAnalyzer audio_analyzer.py audioAnalyzer FFT waveform visualization OBDManager obd_manager.py obdManager Vehicle diagnostics SpotifyManager spotify_manager.py spotifyManager Spotify API AndroidAutoManager android_auto/manager.py androidAutoManager Android Auto DHU PhoneMirrorManager phone_mirror/manager.py phoneMirrorManager Phone screen mirroring ScrcpyCapture phone_mirror/scrcpy_capture.py scrcpyCapture Frame streaming ESP32VolumeManager esp32_volume_manager.py esp32VolumeManager Wireless volume knob BerryIMUManager berryimu_manager.py berryIMU IMU sensor fusion GestureManager gesture_manager.py gestureSensor Gesture recognition NetworkManager network_manager.py networkManager WiFi & updates DownloadManager download_manager.py downloadManager Music download Python-QML Communication Context Properties Backend managers are registered as QML context properties in main.py . This makes them globally accessible in every QML file without imports: # Python (main.py) \u2014 register a manager engine.rootContext().setContextProperty( \""}, {"url": "audio-analyzer.html", "title": "Audio Analyzer", "subtitle": "FFT waveform visualization pipeline: audio decoding, frequency analysis, quality tiers, and the data flow from file to QML", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Pipeline Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "quality-tiers", "text": "Bar Count", "level": 2}, {"id": "decoding", "text": "Audio Decoding", "level": 2}, {"id": "fft-pipeline", "text": "FFT Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-flow", "text": "Data Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Considerations", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-integration", "text": "QML Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "frequency-bands", "text": "Frequency Band Distribution", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Dependencies and Graceful Degradation", "level": 2}], "body": "Audio Analyzer - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Audio Analyzer Audio Analyzer FFT waveform visualization pipeline: audio decoding, frequency analysis, quality tiers, and the data flow from file to QML Pipeline Overview backend/audio_analyzer.py pre-computes FFT visualization data for an entire audio track, then provides time-indexed levels to QML based on playback position. This avoids real-time FFT computation during playback. Audio File (.mp3/.flac/etc.) | v +------------------+ | PyAV Decoder | Decode all frames to PCM samples | (av library) | Convert stereo to mono +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Preprocessing | Normalize to [-1, 1] | | Downsample to ~8 kHz +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | FFT Analysis | Split into 100ms chunks | (NumPy) | Apply Hanning window | | Compute rfft per chunk | | Log-spaced frequency bands +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Normalization | 95th percentile global max | & Quantization | Power curve (^0.6) | | Map to 0-8 integer levels +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Stored as | List of lists: _fft_data[chunk][bar] | _fft_data | ~10 chunks per second of audio +------------------+ | v (during playback) +------------------+ | update_position | Look up chunk by timestamp | (QML callback) | Emit fftDataChanged signal +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | QML Visualizer | Bar heights from 0-8 levels | (MediaRoom) | QML handles smoothing +------------------+ Bar Count The visualizer renders a fixed 96 frequency bars at 60 FPS, the highest-fidelity layout. The waveform is enabled or disabled via the single showWaveformVisualizer setting in MediaRoom \u2014 there are no quality tiers. Audio Decoding Audio files are decoded using the PyAV library (Python bindings for FFmpeg). This supports virtually any audio format: MP3, FLAC, AAC, OGG, WAV, etc. import av container = av. open (file_path) audio_stream = next ( (s for s in container.streams if s.type == 'audio' ), None ) samples = [] sample_rate = audio_stream.rate or 44100 for frame in container. decode (audio_stream): frame_data = frame. to_ndarray () # Stereo to mono: average channels if len (frame_data.shape) > 1 : frame_data = frame_data. mean (axis= 0 ) samples. append (frame_data) container. close () Preprocessing Steps Concatenate \u2014 All decoded frames are joined into a single float32 array Normalize \u2014 Divide by the global maximum absolute value to get a [-1, 1] range Downsample \u2014 Reduce to approximately 8 kHz by taking every Nth sample ( N = sample_rate // 8000 ). This dramatically reduces FFT computation while retaining sufficient frequency information for visualization. # Concatenate and normalize all_samples = np. concatenate (samples). astype (np.float32) max_val = np. max (np. abs (all_samples)) if max_val > 0 : all_samples = all_samples / max_val # Downsample to ~8kHz downsample_factor = max ( 1 , sample_rate // 8000 ) all_samples = all_samples[::downsample_factor] effective_rate = sample_rate // downsample_factor FFT Pipeline The preprocessed audio is split into 100ms chunks (10 chunks per second). Each chunk is analyzed independently: chunk_size = int (effective_rate * 0.1 ) # 100ms chunks num_chunks = len (all_samples) // chunk_size window = np. hanning (chunk_size) # Pre-compute once Per-Chunk Processing Window function \u2014 A Hanning window is applied to reduce spectral leakage at chunk boundaries: windowed = chunk * window FFT computation \u2014 Real FFT via NumPy, taking the magnitude and discarding DC (index 0) and the mirror half: fft = np. abs (np.fft. rfft (windowed)) fft = fft[ 1 : len (fft)// 2 ] Logarithmic frequency bands \u2014 The FFT bins are grouped into num_bars bands using logarithmic spacing. This gives more resolution to lower frequencies (which humans are more sensitive to) and groups higher frequencies together: log_indices = np. logspace ( 0 , np. log10 ( len (fft)), self._num_bars + 1 , dtype= int ) Band averaging \u2014 Each band's magnitude is the mean of its FFT bins Global Normalization (Two-Pass) After computing raw FFT magnitudes for all chunks, a second pass normalizes and quantizes: # Find 95th percentile to avoid outlier spikes all_values = [v for chunk in raw_fft_data for v in chunk if v > 0 ] global_max = np. percentile (all_values, 95 ) # Normalize, apply power curve, quantize to 0-8 for levels in raw_fft_data: for level in levels: norm = level / global_max norm = min ( 1.0 , norm) ** 0.6 # Power curve for visual balance quantized = int (norm * 8 ) # 0-8 integer levels Why 95th percentile? Using the maximum value would compress the entire visualization to accommodate rare peaks. The 95th percentile allows occasional clipping at 8 but keeps the overall visualization dynamic and visually interesting. Why power curve 0.6? The ** 0.6 exponent compresses the dynamic range, boosting quiet passages and taming loud ones. Without it, most bars would hover near zero with occasional spikes. The 0.6 value was tuned for visual balance. Data Flow Once analys"}, {"url": "bottom-bar.html", "title": "Bottom Bar", "subtitle": "The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, volume system, media transport, and dual-orientation support", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "layout", "text": "Layout and Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "orientation", "text": "Dual Orientation", "level": 2}, {"id": "media-controls", "text": "Media Transport Controls", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume", "text": "Volume Slider and Mute", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation", "text": "Navigation Buttons", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle", "text": "Shuffle State", "level": 2}, {"id": "managers", "text": "Manager Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "external-volume", "text": "External Volume Sources", "level": 2}, {"id": "mute-states", "text": "Mute Icon States", "level": 2}, {"id": "animation-pattern", "text": "Button Animation Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "svg-pattern", "text": "SVG Icon Pattern", "level": 2}], "body": "Bottom Bar - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Bottom Bar Bottom Bar The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, volume system, media transport, and dual-orientation support Overview frontend/BottomBar.qml is the persistent control surface that appears at all times. It is a Rectangle with a themed gradient background, containing three logical sections arranged via a Loader that switches between horizontal and vertical layout components. Horizontal Layout (default): +-------------------+------------------+---------------------+ | SECTION 1 | SECTION 2 | SECTION 3 | | Media Controls | Navigation | (Android Auto / | | [<<] [>||] [>>] | [Home] [OBD] | Phone Mirror) | | [Shuffle] [Mute] | [Media] [Gear] | | | [Vol: 50%] | [AA] [PM] | | +-------------------+------------------+---------------------+ Vertical Layout (side mode): +--------+ | [Home] | | [OBD] | | [Media]| | [Gear] | | [AA] | | [PM] | |--------| | [<<] | | [>||] | | [>>] | | [Shuf] | | [Mute] | | [Vol] | +--------+ Layout and Structure The BottomBar is a Rectangle with a gradient background that adapts direction based on orientation: Rectangle { id : bottomBar gradient : Gradient { orientation : isVertical ? Gradient.Horizontal : Gradient.Vertical GradientStop { position : 1.5 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientStart } GradientStop { position : 0.0 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientEnd } } required property StackView stackView required property Window mainWindow } A Loader at the top level switches between two complete layout components: Loader { anchors.fill : parent sourceComponent : isVertical ? verticalLayoutComponent : horizontalLayoutComponent } The horizontal layout uses a RowLayout with three sections. The vertical layout uses a ColumnLayout . Both contain the same controls, just arranged differently. Section 1: Media Controls (Horizontal Mode) Takes 40% of width when visible, or 10% when hidden (for symmetry). Visibility is controlled by settingsManager.showBottomBarMediaControls . Section 2: Navigation (Center) Uses Layout.fillWidth: true to fill remaining space, with navigation buttons centered via a RowLayout . Dual Orientation The isVertical property controls whether the bar appears at the bottom (horizontal) or left side (vertical) of the window: property bool isVertical: settingsManager && settingsManager.bottomBarOrientation === \"side\" The updateLayout() function reconfigures anchors when orientation changes: function updateLayout () { if (isVertical) { anchors.bottom = undefined anchors.right = undefined anchors.left = parent.left anchors.top = parent.top width = parent.width * 0.1 height = parent.height } else { anchors.top = undefined anchors.right = undefined anchors.left = parent.left anchors.bottom = parent.bottom width = parent.width height = parent.height * App.Spacing.bottomBarHeightPercent } } Anchor Release Pattern: Anchors are released (set to undefined ) before new ones are assigned. This prevents binding loops that occur when conflicting anchor constraints are temporarily active. Parent size changes are also tracked via Connections to ensure correct sizing after window resizes. Media Transport Controls Each media button follows a consistent pattern using Control with an SVG image and ColorOverlay : Control { id : previousButtonControl implicitWidth : App.Spacing.bottomBarPreviousButtonWidth implicitHeight : App.Spacing.bottomBarPreviousButtonHeight // Press animation scale : mouseAreaPrev.pressed ? 0.8 : 1.0 opacity : mouseAreaPrev.pressed ? 0.7 : 1.0 Behavior on scale { NumberAnimation { duration : 200 easing.type : Easing.OutBack easing.overshoot : 1.1 } } contentItem : Item { Image { id : previousButtonImage source : \"./assets/previous_button.svg\" visible : false // Hidden; used as ColorOverlay source } ColorOverlay { source : previousButtonImage color : App.Style.bottomBarPreviousButton } } } Enlarged Hit Areas Every button wraps its MouseArea in an invisible Item that is significantly larger than the visual icon (typically 2.5x width, 3x height). This improves touch accuracy on car touchscreens: Item { id : previousButtonClickArea anchors.centerIn : parent width : parent.width * 2.5 height : parent.height * 3.0 MouseArea { id : mouseAreaPrev anchors.fill : parent onClicked : useSpotify ? spotifyManager. previous_track () : mediaManager. previous_track () } } Play/Pause Button The play/pause button dynamically switches its icon based on playback state: source : { var isPlaying = useSpotify ? (spotifyManager && spotifyManager. is_playing ()) : (mediaManager && mediaManager. is_playing ()) return isPlaying ? \"./assets/pause_button.svg\" : \"./assets/play_button.svg\" } Volume Slider and Mute Volume Display The volume percentage is shown as a small text label. Clicking it opens a full-width popup with a large slider: Popup { id : popupSlider width : parent.Window.width height : App.Spacing. dp ( 140 ) anchors.centerIn : Overlay.overlay modal : true closePolicy : Popup.CloseOnEscape | Popup.CloseOnPressOutside } L"}, {"url": "building.html", "title": "Building &amp; Distribution", "subtitle": "PyInstaller build process, platform-specific packaging, CI/CD workflow, and distribution formats.", "headings": [{"id": "quick-start", "text": "Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "build-script", "text": "Unified Build Script", "level": 2}, {"id": "spec-file", "text": "PyInstaller Spec File", "level": 2}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "macos", "text": "macOS Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux", "text": "Linux Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci-cd", "text": "GitHub Actions CI/CD", "level": 2}, {"id": "android", "text": "Android APK Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "text": "Build Troubleshooting", "level": 2}], "body": "Building & Distribution - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Building & Distribution Building & Distribution PyInstaller build process, platform-specific packaging, CI/CD workflow, and distribution formats. Quick Start # Build for current platform (creates venv, installs deps, builds) python build_scripts/build.py # Clean build artifacts only python build_scripts/build.py --clean # Clean then rebuild python build_scripts/build.py --clean --build-after-clean Build Location: The build script uses the system temp directory ( /tmp/OCTAVE_build and /tmp/OCTAVE_dist ) for intermediate files to avoid Dropbox sync issues. Final output is copied to dist/ in the project root. Unified Build Script build_scripts/build.py The unified build script handles all platforms with a single entry point. It performs these steps: Setup: Creates a virtual environment in venv/ if not present, installs requirements.txt and PyInstaller Clean: Removes build/ , dist/ , __pycache__/ , and .pyc files recursively Build: Runs PyInstaller with build_scripts/octave.spec Package: Creates platform-specific installer (Inno Setup / DMG / AppImage) Copy: Copies build output from temp dir to project dist/ folder Platform-Specific Build Scripts Script Platform Description build_scripts/build_windows.bat Windows Windows batch file alternative to build.py build_scripts/build_macos.sh macOS Shell script for macOS builds build_scripts/build_linux.sh Linux Shell script for Linux builds PyInstaller Spec File build_scripts/octave.spec The spec file defines exactly what gets bundled into the executable: Data Files Collected Source Contents Notes frontend/ All QML files, assets, fonts Excludes -src directories (source design files) backend/ JSON and TXT data files Excludes __pycache__ , temp , media , dotfiles tools/scrcpy/ Bundled scrcpy executable For phone mirroring, if present PySide6 QML Qt Quick modules Collected via collect_data_files('PySide6') Hidden Imports The spec file declares hidden imports for all PySide6 modules, backend dependencies, and platform-specific keyring backends: hidden_imports = [ 'PySide6.QtCore' , 'PySide6.QtGui' , 'PySide6.QtQml' , 'PySide6.QtQuick' , 'PySide6.QtQuickControls2' , 'PySide6.QtMultimedia' , 'PySide6.QtNetwork' , 'mutagen' , 'spotipy' , 'obd' , 'numpy' , 'scipy' , 'keyring' , 'PIL' , # + platform-specific keyring backends # + all PySide6 submodules via collect_submodules() ] Excluded Modules To reduce bundle size, these are excluded: tkinter , matplotlib , cv2 , pandas , pytest . Platform EXE Differences Setting Windows macOS Linux Name OCTAVE OCTAVE octave (lowercase) Console False (GUI) False (GUI) False (GUI) UPX True True True argv_emulation False True False Icon icon.ico icon.icns (none) Windows Build Windows Requirements Python 3.8+ (Optional) Inno Setup 6 for creating the installer Output dist\\OCTAVE\\OCTAVE.exe - Standalone executable (portable, distribute the whole folder) dist\\OCTAVE_Setup_1.0.0.exe - Inno Setup installer (if Inno Setup is installed) Inno Setup Installer The installer script at build_scripts/installer_windows.iss creates a professional Windows installer with Start Menu shortcuts and uninstaller. macOS Build macOS Requirements Python 3.8+ Xcode Command Line Tools ( xcode-select --install ) Output dist/OCTAVE.app - Application bundle dist/OCTAVE_1.0.0.dmg - DMG installer with Applications symlink App Bundle Info.plist The spec file configures the macOS app bundle with: bundle_identifier: 'com.waybettersolutions.octave' NSHighResolutionCapable: True NSRequiresAquaSystemAppearance: False # Dark mode support LSMinimumSystemVersion: '10.14.0' # Bluetooth and microphone usage descriptions included DMG Creation The build script creates a DMG using hdiutil create in UDZO (compressed) format, with an Applications symlink for drag-to-install. Code Signing: Unsigned macOS apps trigger Gatekeeper warnings. Users can bypass with xattr -cr /Applications/OCTAVE.app . For distribution, sign with a Developer ID certificate. Linux Build Linux Requirements Python 3.8+ Build essentials: sudo apt install build-essential wget (for automatic appimagetool download) Output dist/OCTAVE/octave - Standalone executable dist/OCTAVE-x86_64.AppImage - Portable single-file AppImage AppImage Structure The build script creates an AppDir with proper structure: OCTAVE.AppDir/ AppRun # Shell script entry point octave.desktop # Desktop entry file usr/bin/ # All PyInstaller output usr/share/applications/ # Desktop entry copy usr/share/icons/ # App icon The AppRun script sets critical environment variables: export QT_QPA_PLATFORM_PLUGIN_PATH= \"${HERE}/usr/bin/PySide6/Qt/plugins/platforms\" export QML2_IMPORT_PATH= \"${HERE}/usr/bin/PySide6/Qt/qml\" GitHub Actions CI/CD .github/workflows/build.yml Triggers Manual: workflow_dispatch (trigger from GitHub UI) Version tags: Push tags matching v* (e.g., v1.0.0 ) Jobs Job Runner Artifacts Retention build-windows windows-latest OCTAVE-Windows (folder zip) 30 days build-macos macos-latest OCTAVE-macOS"}, {"url": "components.html", "title": "Components", "subtitle": "Reusable QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, LazyImage, GForceDisplay, SensorCard, OBDParameterModel, TerminalFeedback, and CornerBrackets", "headings": [{"id": "album-art-carousel", "text": "AlbumArtCarousel", "level": 2}, {"id": "coverflow-card", "text": "CoverflowCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "lazy-image", "text": "LazyImage", "level": 2}, {"id": "gforce-display", "text": "GForceDisplay", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensor-card", "text": "SensorCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-parameter-model", "text": "OBDParameterModel", "level": 2}, {"id": "terminal-feedback", "text": "TerminalFeedback", "level": 2}, {"id": "corner-brackets", "text": "CornerBrackets", "level": 2}], "body": "Components - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Components Components Reusable QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, LazyImage, GForceDisplay, SensorCard, OBDParameterModel, TerminalFeedback, and CornerBrackets AlbumArtCarousel frontend/AlbumArtCarousel.qml is the album art display component used in MediaRoom. It supports two categories of transition animations and configurable visual modes. Animation Modes Mode Condition Styles Available Single-card previewEnabled: false Crossfade, Slide, Vinyl Lift, Dissolve, Flip 3D preview previewEnabled: true Coverflow, Conveyor, Stack, Depth, Swing When 3D preview is active, previous and next side cards are visible at idle, and all three cards participate in the transition animation. Public API // Navigation direction property int direction: 1 // Art sources (center, previous, next) property url artSource: \"\" property url prevArtSource: \"\" property url nextArtSource: \"\" // Animation state property bool animBusy: false // Transition configuration property string transitionStyle: \"Crossfade\" property string transition3DStyle: \"Coverflow\" // Visual settings property bool previewEnabled: false property bool roundedArt: false property real artRadius: 0 property bool vinylMode: false property real sideCardAngle: 30 property real sideCardOpacity: 0.4 property bool showShadow: false // Vinyl rotation tracking property real playbackPosition: 0 property real playbackDuration: 0 // Dissolve state (exposed for background sync) readonly property bool dissolveActive readonly property real dissolveProgress readonly property url dissolveNewArt // Signals signal animationFinished () signal settled () signal artClicked () signal artLoadFailed () Animation Constants Animation Duration Key Parameter Crossfade 250ms Scale: 0.97 Slide 280ms Distance: 80px Vinyl Lift 180ms + 60ms pause Scale: 0.85 Dissolve 900ms Grid: 20px, Smoothness: 0.1 Flip 350ms Full 3D Y-axis rotation 3D modes 350ms Side offset: 0.42, Side scale: 0.72 Dissolve Sync: The dissolve animation exposes its progress and new art source as readonly properties so the parent (MediaRoom) can sync the blurred background transition with the album art dissolve. CoverflowCard frontend/CoverflowCard.qml is a single album art card with 3D perspective transform, used by AlbumArtCarousel for coverflow-style animations. Properties // Coverflow transform properties (animated by parent) property real cfOffset: 0 // X translation property real cfAngle: 0 // Y-axis rotation angle property real cfScale: 1.0 // Scale factor property real cfOpacity: 1.0 // Opacity // Image configuration property url artSource: \"\" property real artSize: width property bool asynchronous: true // Masking modes property bool roundedArt: false property bool vinylMode: false property real artRadius: 0 property Item roundedMask: null // Shared mask texture property Item vinylMask: null // Shared mask texture // Vinyl rotation (center card only) property real vinylAngle: 0 // Drop shadow (center card only) property bool showShadow: false 3D Transform The card applies a Translate and Rotation transform to create the coverflow perspective effect: transform : [ Translate { x : root.cfOffset }, Rotation { axis { x : 0 ; y : 1 ; z : 0 } angle : root.cfAngle origin.x : root.width / 2 origin.y : root.height / 2 } ] GPU Optimization: Mask items ( roundedMask , vinylMask ) are passed in from the parent rather than created per-card, avoiding duplicate GPU texture allocations. LazyImage frontend/LazyImage.qml is a visibility-aware image loader for use in lists and grids. It only loads its source when the component is visible. Properties property string source // Image URL/path property bool rounded: false // Enable rounded corners property int fidelity: 60 // sourceSize (lower = faster load) property bool smoothing: false // Enable smooth filtering property bool mipmap: false // Enable mipmapping property bool antialiasing: false property real quality: 0.0 // Image decode quality property int cacheLimit: 2048 // Cache size in KB Implementation Image { source : root.visible ? root.source : \"\" asynchronous : true cache : true sourceSize.width : root.fidelity sourceSize.height : root.fidelity visible : status === Image.Ready } The key optimization is source: root.visible ? root.source : \"\" . When a LazyImage scrolls off-screen, its source is cleared, freeing the decoded image from memory. The low fidelity default (60px) means thumbnail-sized images decode very fast. Usage Example LazyImage { source : mediaManager. get_album_art (filename) fidelity : 60 width : App.Spacing.mediaPlayerAlbumArtSize height : width } GForceDisplay frontend/GForceDisplay.qml is a specialized G-force visualization card for the SensorMenu. Properties property real lateralG: 0 property real longitudinalG: 0 property real magnitude: 0 property bool connected: false property int decimalPlaces: 2 property real maxG: 1.5 Features Trail history \u2014 A ListModel stores the last 30 G-force readings (sampled every 50m"}, {"url": "development.html", "title": "Development Guide", "subtitle": "Dev mode, mock OBD, dev tools, MCP server, performance profiling, command server, and how to add new features.", "headings": [{"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Dev Mode Setup &amp; Usage", "level": 2}, {"id": "mock-obd", "text": "Mock OBD Manager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tools", "text": "DevToolsPanel", "level": 2}, {"id": "mcp-server", "text": "MCP Server for Remote Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "profiling", "text": "Performance Profiling", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command Server (TCP)", "level": 2}, {"id": "screenshots", "text": "Screenshot Tool", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-features", "text": "How to Add New Features", "level": 2}, {"id": "testing", "text": "Testing &amp; Linting", "level": 2}], "body": "Development Guide - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Development Guide Development Guide Dev mode, mock OBD, dev tools, MCP server, performance profiling, command server, and how to add new features. Dev Mode Setup & Usage Dev mode provides simulated vehicle data and keyboard controls for testing without a physical OBD-II adapter. # Launch dev mode python -m dev.main_dev The dev/ directory is gitignored and fully isolated from production code. Dev mode: Replaces OBDManager with MockOBDManager Adds a KeyboardController exposed to QML as devKeyboardController Sets devMode = true as a QML context property Loads dev/DevMain.qml with keyboard handler overlay Adds DevToolsManager for screenshots and diagnostics Keyboard Controls Key Action Type W / Up Accelerate (hold for continuous) Continuous S / Down Brake (hold for continuous) Continuous Space Quick rev (throttle blip to 70%) Instant E Toggle engine on/off Toggle Q Downshift Instant R Upshift Instant 1 - 6 Set specific gear Instant F1 Simulate cold start Scenario F2 Simulate overheating Scenario F3 Simulate low fuel Scenario F4 Reset to normal conditions Scenario Escape Quit application Instant Dev Mode Architecture main_dev.py | +-- MockOBDManager (replaces OBDManager) +-- KeyboardController (handles key events from QML) +-- DevToolsManager (screenshots, diagnostics) +-- DevMain.qml (wraps Main.qml with keyboard handler) | +-- All regular managers (MediaManager, SpotifyManager, etc.) Mock OBD Manager dev/mock_obd_manager.py The MockOBDManager provides the same Signal interface as the real OBDManager but generates simulated data based on keyboard input and physics modeling. Signal Compatibility Emits all 87+ OBD signals identical to the real manager: rpmChanged(float) , speedMPHChanged(float) , coolantTempChanged(float) , etc. Plus all connection status signals. QML code works identically with both. Simulation Features Physics-based RPM/speed: Throttle input drives RPM, which drives speed through gear ratios Gear simulation: 6 gears with realistic ratios, manual shifting via Q/R Temperature modeling: Coolant and oil temp rise with engine load, cool at idle Fuel consumption: Fuel level decreases proportional to load Scenario presets: Cold start (low coolant, rich fuel), overheating (high temps, low voltage), low fuel Key Methods set_throttle (percent) # 0-100 throttle input set_brake (percent) # 0-100 brake input rev_engine (percent) # Quick throttle blip toggle_engine () # Engine on/off upshift () / downshift () # Gear changes simulate_cold_start () # Preset scenario simulate_overheating () # Preset scenario simulate_low_fuel () # Preset scenario reset_to_normal () # Reset all values DevToolsPanel dev/dev_tools_manager.py & dev/DevToolsPanel.qml The DevToolsManager provides dev-only tools exposed to QML as devToolsManager . Screenshot Capture Uses Qt's grabWindow() for cross-platform screenshots (works on Wayland and X11): Saves to dev/screenshots/output/ Maintains a manifest.json tracking all screenshots with metadata Emits hideOverlaysRequested / showOverlaysRequested signals to hide dev UI for clean captures Signals Signal Parameters Description screenshotStatusChanged str \"idle\", \"capturing\", \"done\", \"error\" screenshotOutputChanged str Accumulated terminal-style output screenshotCountChanged int Total screenshots in manifest isCapturingChanged bool Whether a capture is in progress hideOverlaysRequested (none) QML should hide dev overlays showOverlaysRequested (none) QML should show dev overlays again MCP Server for Remote Control dev/mcp_server.py The MCP (Model Context Protocol) server lets an AI agent (like Claude Code) remote-control OCTAVE for automated testing. Setup # Install MCP dependency pip install mcp # Start OCTAVE with command server enabled python main.py --profile # In another terminal, start the MCP server python dev/mcp_server.py How It Works AI Agent (Claude Code) | v MCP Server (dev/mcp_server.py) | TCP socket (127.0.0.1:9876) v Command Server (backend/command_server.py) | QMetaObject.invokeMethod v OCTAVE Managers (media, settings, OBD, etc.) Available MCP Tools Tool Arguments Description ping() - Check if OCTAVE is running navigate(page) page name Navigate to a specific page (home, media_room, obd, settings, etc.) go_back() - Navigate back in the stack go_home() - Return to home screen list_actions() - List all available actions Plus media controls (next_track, toggle_play, etc.), Spotify controls, OBD controls, and settings manipulation tools. See dev/mcp_server.py for the full list. Benchmark Tool: dev/mcp_benchmark.py provides automated benchmark sequences that the MCP server can run for repeatable performance testing. Performance Profiling backend/perf_monitor.py Launch with Profiling python main.py --profile What Gets Measured Metric Method Threshold Event-loop latency QTimer(0) probe measures time between iterations <5ms green, <16ms yellow, >16ms red GIL contention Background thread measures jitter vs expected 10ms sleep Higher = more"}, {"url": "download-manager.html", "title": "Download Manager", "subtitle": "Music search and download system using Spotify metadata and YouTube audio", "headings": [], "body": "Download Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Download Manager Download Manager Music search and download system using Spotify metadata and YouTube audio Overview The DownloadManager provides music search and download functionality within OCTAVE. It combines Spotify metadata with YouTube audio sources to download high-quality music files. Defined in backend/download_manager.py , it wraps the internal music_dl engine (a fork of spotdl) and exposes search/download operations to QML via the standard OCTAVE manager pattern. No Spotify Credentials Required The search system uses YouTube Music ( ytmusicapi ) by default, so Spotify credentials are optional. When provided, they enable Spotify URL resolution (track, album, playlist URLs) for richer metadata. System Architecture DownloadManager (QObject) | |-- MusicDL engine (lazy init) | |-- SpotifyClient (optional, via spotipy) | |-- Downloader | | |-- yt-dlp (audio extraction) | | |-- ffmpeg (transcoding) | | |-- progress_handler.py (callbacks) | | | |-- utils/ | | |-- search.py ytmusicapi + Spotify URL parsing | | |-- matching.py fuzzy title matching (rapidfuzz) | | |-- metadata.py ID3/M4A tag writing | | |-- formatter.py filename sanitization | | |-- ffmpeg.py audio conversion | | |-- spotify.py Spotify API client | | | |-- types/ | | |-- song.py Song data class | | |-- album.py Album container | | |-- playlist.py Playlist container | | |-- options.py Downloader settings TypedDict | | | |-- providers/audio/ Audio source providers | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (2 workers) |-- QTimer (200ms polling) |-- Thread-safe progress queue The music_dl Engine The music_dl package lives in backend/music_dl/ and is a forked and trimmed version of spotdl . It provides the core download pipeline independent of the Qt/QML layer. Entry Point: MusicDL Class The MusicDL class in backend/music_dl/__init__.py is the main API: class MusicDL : def __init__ ( self , client_id, client_secret, headless= False , downloader_settings= None ): self .has_spotify = bool (client_id and client_secret) if self .has_spotify: SpotifyClient .init(client_id=client_id, ...) self .downloader = Downloader (settings=downloader_settings) def search ( self , query): # -> List[Song] def download ( self , song): # -> Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]] def download_songs ( self , songs): # -> List[Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]]] Sub-Module Reference Module Path Purpose download/downloader.py backend/music_dl/download/downloader.py Core download + convert logic, yt-dlp integration, multi-threaded batch downloads download/progress_handler.py backend/music_dl/download/progress_handler.py Download progress tracking with callback support types/song.py backend/music_dl/types/song.py Song data class with from_url() and from_missing_data() constructors types/album.py backend/music_dl/types/album.py Album container -- resolves to list of Songs types/playlist.py backend/music_dl/types/playlist.py Playlist container -- resolves to list of Songs types/options.py backend/music_dl/types/options.py TypedDict definitions for downloader settings utils/search.py backend/music_dl/utils/search.py YouTube Music search, Spotify URL parsing, query routing utils/matching.py backend/music_dl/utils/matching.py Fuzzy string matching for finding best YouTube matches (uses rapidfuzz ) utils/metadata.py backend/music_dl/utils/metadata.py ID3/M4A tag embedding (title, artist, album art, etc.) utils/formatter.py backend/music_dl/utils/formatter.py Filename sanitization and output path template formatting utils/ffmpeg.py backend/music_dl/utils/ffmpeg.py FFmpeg wrapper for audio conversion (MP3, FLAC, OGG, OPUS, M4A) utils/spotify.py backend/music_dl/utils/spotify.py Spotify API client singleton (wraps spotipy ) providers/audio/ backend/music_dl/providers/audio/ Audio source provider plugins for YouTube Search Flow When the user types a query in the QML search bar and calls downloadManager.search(query) , the following flow executes: Text Queries (Default Path) Lazy-init engine -- _ensure_music_dl() creates the MusicDL instance if not already initialized, loading Spotify credentials from the settings manager keychain. YouTube Music search -- Calls ytmusicapi.search(query, filter=\"songs\", limit=20) via the get_ytm_client() singleton from backend/music_dl/utils/search.py . Parse results -- Each YTMusic result is transformed into a standardized result dict containing: name, artist, artists, album_name, duration (seconds), year, cover_url, song_id (YouTube video ID), and a YouTube Music URL. Enhance cover art URLs -- Google CDN thumbnail URLs are rewritten to request 800x800 high-resolution versions: if \"lh3.googleusercontent.com\" in cover_url: cover_url = re.sub( r\"=w\\d+-h\\d+.*$\" , \"=w800-h800-l90-rj\" , cover_url) Mark downloaded -- The _mark_downloaded() method scans the media library to tag results that already exist on disk (sets is_downloaded: true and downloaded_playlist name). Emit to QML -- Results are JSON-serialized and emitted via searchResults signal."}, {"url": "frontend-overview.html", "title": "Frontend Overview", "subtitle": "QML architecture, Main.qml structure, page routing, and the bridge between Python managers and the UI", "headings": [{"id": "architecture", "text": "QML Architecture Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "main-qml", "text": "Main.qml Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "window-management", "text": "Window Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-routing", "text": "Page Routing Mechanism", "level": 2}, {"id": "import-path", "text": "Import Path Setup", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Accessing Python Managers (Context Properties)", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-loading", "text": "Dynamic Font Loading", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "shift-light", "text": "Shift Light Logic", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-connections", "text": "Settings Manager Connections", "level": 2}, {"id": "drop-shadows", "text": "Drop Shadows", "level": 2}, {"id": "initialization", "text": "Initialization Sequence", "level": 2}], "body": "Frontend Overview - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Frontend Overview Frontend Overview QML architecture, Main.qml structure, page routing, and the bridge between Python managers and the UI QML Architecture Overview OCTAVE's frontend is built with Qt Quick 2.15 (QML). The QML layer handles all visual presentation, animations, and user interaction. Business logic lives in backend manager classes \u2014 C++ / Qt 6 in src/ (the shipped binary) or the parallel Python / PySide6 backend in backend/ \u2014 that are exposed to QML as context properties . The frontend works identically against either backend. ApplicationWindow (Main.qml) +-------------------------------------------+ | mainContainer (Item) | | +---------------------------------------+| | | StackView (page navigation) || | | +-----------------------------------+|| | | | MainMenu.qml (initialItem) ||| | | | MediaPlayer.qml ||| | | | MediaRoom.qml ||| | | | OBDMenu.qml ||| | | | SettingsMenu.qml ||| | | | ... ||| | | +-----------------------------------+|| | +---------------------------------------+| | | BottomBar.qml (persistent nav+media) || | +---------------------------------------+| | | globalFullScreenFlash (z: 999999) || | +---------------------------------------+| +-------------------------------------------+ Key architectural points: Single window \u2014 one ApplicationWindow hosts everything StackView navigation \u2014 pages are pushed/popped onto a stack; no router library Persistent BottomBar \u2014 always visible below (or beside) the StackView Singleton design tokens \u2014 Style.qml , Spacing.qml , and EnvironmentTheme.qml are pragma Singleton objects No page transitions \u2014 all StackView enter/exit transitions are set to null for performance Main.qml Structure The entry point file frontend/Main.qml is an ApplicationWindow that orchestrates the entire UI. Here is its top-level declaration: import QtQuick 2.15 import QtQuick.Controls 2.15 import QtQuick.Layouts 1.15 import Qt.labs.folderlistmodel 2.15 import \".\" as App ApplicationWindow { id : mainWindow visible : true title : deviceName minimumWidth : 400 minimumHeight : 300 font.family : App.Style.fontFamily !== \"\" ? App.Style.fontFamily : systemDefaultFont } The window stores a captured systemDefaultFont at startup, before any custom fonts are applied. This ensures there is always a valid fallback. Key Properties Property Source Purpose deviceName settingsManager.deviceName Window title theme settingsManager.themeSetting Current color theme name screenWidth / screenHeight settingsManager Persisted window dimensions isVerticalLayout settingsManager.bottomBarOrientation Side bar vs bottom bar mode currentRpm obdManager.rpmChanged signal Live RPM for shift light overlay shiftLightFlags settingsManager (JSON array) RPM threshold + color flag definitions Window Management Window dimensions are persisted bidirectionally. On startup, Main.qml reads saved dimensions from settingsManager . On resize, it writes them back: onWidthChanged : { if (settingsManager && width > 0 && width >= minimumWidth) { settingsManager. save_screen_width (width) screenWidth = width App.Spacing. updateDimensions (width, height) } } The Spacing.updateDimensions() call recalculates all percentage-based layout values. Window state (fullscreen, maximized, normal) is also saved and restored: // Restore window state let savedState = settingsManager. get_window_state () if (savedState === \"fullscreen\" ) mainWindow.visibility = Window.FullScreen else if (savedState === \"maximized\" ) mainWindow.visibility = Window.Maximized Cleanup on Close: The onClosing handler saves playback state, clears temp files, and cleans up Spotify before the window closes. Page Routing Mechanism OCTAVE uses Qt's StackView for page navigation. The initial item is MainMenu : StackView { id : stackView objectName : \"stackView\" initialItem : MainMenu { stackView : stackView windowWidth : mainWindow.width windowHeight : mainWindow.height } // Disable all transitions for performance pushEnter : null pushExit : null popEnter : null popExit : null } Pages push themselves onto the stack by creating components dynamically. For example, navigating to OBD from the BottomBar: var component = Qt. createComponent ( \"OBDMenu.qml\" ) var page = component. createObject (stackView, { stackView : bottomBar.stackView, mainWindow : stackView.parent.Window.window }) stackView. push (page) The navigateTo() function provides a complete page map for remote/programmatic navigation: function navigateTo (pageName) { var pageMap = { \"MainMenu.qml\" : { file: \"MainMenu.qml\" , props: { ... } }, \"MediaRoom.qml\" : { file: \"MediaRoom.qml\" , props: { ... } }, \"MediaPlayer.qml\" : { file: \"MediaPlayer.qml\" , props: { ... } }, \"OBDMenu.qml\" : { file: \"OBDMenu.qml\" , props: { ... } }, // ... 12 pages total } } There are also navigateHome() (pops to root) and navigateBack() (pops one) helper functions, primarily used by a Python command server for performance testing. StackView Anchoring The StackView adapts its anchoring based"}, {"url": "gauges-dashboards.html", "title": "Gauges &amp; Dashboards", "subtitle": "Reusable gauge primitives and pre-built dashboards for live OBD data. Accessed from the \"Dashboards\" pill in the top-right of the OBD menu.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "primitives", "text": "Gauge primitives", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboards", "text": "Built-in dashboards", "level": 2}, {"id": "editor", "text": "Dashboard editor", "level": 2}, {"id": "authoring", "text": "Authoring new gauges &amp; dashboards", "level": 2}], "body": "Gauges & Dashboards - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Gauges & Dashboards Gauges & Dashboards Reusable gauge primitives and pre-built dashboards for live OBD data. Accessed from the \"Dashboards\" pill in the top-right of the OBD menu. Overview OCTAVE ships with a small library of gauge primitives under frontend/gauges/ and a set of pre-built dashboards defined as JSON specs under frontend/dashboards/presets/ , rendered by frontend/dashboards/DashboardRenderer.qml . Dashboards render inline inside the OBD menu \u2014 the default view is the parameter-card grid, and selecting a dashboard swaps the grid for the chosen layout in place. Selection is persisted as the activeDashboard setting. DashboardManager (C++ and Python, full parity) enumerates built-in presets plus user-authored dashboards from the OS config dir and exposes save / delete / duplicate. Entry point: a \"Dashboards\" pill at the top-right of the OBD menu opens a modal chooser listing \"Parameter Cards\" plus each registered dashboard. Tapping a choice swaps the content and closes the popup. Gauges bind to the OBDParameterModel singleton by paramId (e.g. \"RPM\" , \"SPEED\" ) \u2014 title, unit, min and max are auto-populated from the model, and the live value updates reactively as the backend polls each PID. No manual signal wiring is required. Gauge primitives Primitive Use for CircularGauge Arc-sweep dial with optional tick marks, needle, redline and center readout. Flagship gauge for RPM, speed, coolant, etc. BarGauge Horizontal or vertical filled bar. Good for fuel, throttle, intake temps. Supports a warnAbove threshold that recolors the fill. LinearGauge Horizontal scale with tick marks and a sliding indicator. Best for bidirectional ranges that straddle zero (fuel trim, timing advance, EGR error). DigitalReadout Big numeric value with subdued label and unit. Use as the dominant element on a dashboard when an arc is overkill. ArcGauge Half-dial (180\u00b0 top arc with readout below the chord). Compact, wide-aspect alternative to CircularGauge for speedometer-style layouts. Shares the same arc/tick/needle/redline engine. SparklineGauge Rolling history line showing recent values. Use when motion matters more than absolute value \u2014 boost, load, fuel trim. Timer-sampled so the x-axis scale stays stable; autoScale rescales the y-axis to the visible window for small-swing PIDs. WarningLight Binary tell-tale indicator bound to a triggerAbove / triggerBelow threshold. Composable \u2014 drop several into a row for an idiot-light strip, or overlay one atop a larger gauge as a critical alert. GForceGauge 2D G-force dot with concentric rings, fading trail and total-G readout. Self-binding (no PID) \u2014 reads lateral/longitudinal acceleration from the BerryIMU. CompassGauge Rotating compass card with cardinal letters, fixed lubber line, and degrees/cardinal readout. Self-binding (no PID) \u2014 reads the BerryIMU magnetometer heading. NowPlayingWidget Album art + title + artist + progress for the active media source (local or Spotify, following the app-wide source setting). Self-binding, display-only. MediaControlsWidget Previous / play-pause / next transport buttons driving the active media source. Interactive in rendered dashboards. Sensor data also flows through the regular PID system: the BerryIMU publishes PITCH , ROLL , HEADING , ALTITUDE , ACCEL_MAG , LATERAL_G , LONGITUDINAL_G and BARO_TEMP as parameters in OBDParameterModel , so any gauge can bind them (lateral G on a sparkline, pitch on a linear gauge\u2026), they appear in the editor's PID picker, and demo mode sweeps them like any other PID. Built-in dashboards Dashboard Layout Sport Huge digital speed readout + large RPM dial with redline, vitals strip (coolant / oil / fuel / throttle) along the bottom. Minimal Three round circular gauges across the screen: Speed, RPM (with redline), Fuel. Full Grid 4\u00d72 grid of compact circular gauges covering Speed, RPM, Coolant, Oil, Throttle, Engine Load, Fuel, and System Voltage. Performance Arc speedometer + rolling sparklines for load / boost / trim, with warning lights for overtemp and overrev. Dashboard editor Custom dashboards are built in-app , no file editing required. In the dashboard chooser, tap + New to start from a blank grid, or use the action buttons on each card: Copy duplicates any dashboard (the only way to derive from a read-only built-in), and Edit / Delete appear on user-authored dashboards. Deleting asks for a confirming second tap. The editor is hybrid tap-and-drag, designed for an in-car touchscreen: Tap an empty grid cell (marked + ) to open the widget palette and place a primitive there. The PID picker opens immediately after placing. Drag a placed widget to move it \u2014 it snaps to the grid, with a live green/red highlight showing whether the drop is valid. Invalid drops bounce back. Tap a placed widget to select it. A floating panel (docked to whichever side is away from the selection, so the canvas never rescales) edits its PID (searchable picker, filtered to the kinds the wid"}, {"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting Started", "subtitle": "Installation, setup, and first run on all supported platforms.", "headings": [{"id": "requirements", "text": "System Requirements", "level": 2}, {"id": "quick-start", "text": "Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "setup-script", "text": "What setup.py Does", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-linux", "text": "Linux Installation", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux-arch", "text": "Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-debian", "text": "Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-fedora", "text": "Fedora / RHEL / CentOS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-rpi", "text": "Raspberry Pi", "level": 3}, {"id": "platform-windows", "text": "Windows Installation", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-macos", "text": "macOS Installation", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-android", "text": "Android Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-wsl", "text": "WSL Notes", "level": 2}, {"id": "running", "text": "Running OCTAVE", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Developer Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Python Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "directory-structure", "text": "Directory Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "first-steps", "text": "First Steps After Install", "level": 2}], "body": "Getting Started - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Getting Started Getting Started Installation, setup, and first run on all supported platforms. System Requirements Requirement Minimum Notes Python 3.8+ Desktop developer backend only \u2014 not used for Android (C++/Qt) builds OS Windows 10+, macOS 10.14+, Linux Raspberry Pi OS fully supported Display Any resolution Auto-scales based on screen height (720p baseline). Scale factor clamped to 0.4\u20133.0. Audio PulseAudio / ALSA / CoreAudio / DirectSound Required for media playback. Qt uses the platform's native audio. Network Optional Required for Spotify, music download, and update checking. Local playback works offline. Quick Start The fastest path from zero to running: # Clone the repository git clone https://github.com/waybettersolutions/octave.git cd octave # Setup and run (creates venv, installs deps, launches app) python setup.py That's it. The setup.py script handles everything automatically. If you only want to install without launching: python setup.py --no-run To run manually after setup: # Linux / macOS source venv/bin/activate python main.py # Windows venv\\Scripts\\activate python main.py What setup.py Does setup.py The setup script performs these steps in order: Detects your platform \u2014 Identifies Windows, macOS, Linux, or Raspberry Pi. On Linux, detects the distro family (Arch, Debian, Fedora) by reading /etc/os-release or falling back to package manager detection ( pacman , apt , dnf ). Checks Python version \u2014 Ensures Python 3.8 or higher is available. Exits with a clear error if not. Installs system dependencies (Linux only) \u2014 Uses the appropriate package manager to install required system libraries: audio (PulseAudio/ALSA), OpenGL/EGL, XCB platform plugins, X11 libs, fonts, D-Bus, and Qt6 base packages. Creates a virtual environment \u2014 Uses Python's built-in venv module to create an isolated environment at ./venv/ . If an existing venv is incompatible (wrong platform or broken pip), it is automatically recreated. Installs Python dependencies \u2014 Upgrades pip, then installs everything from requirements.txt into the venv. Launches the application \u2014 Unless --no-run is passed, replaces the setup process with the OCTAVE app via os.execv() . Linux Installation Linux is the primary development and deployment platform. The setup script automatically installs system-level dependencies via your distro's package manager. Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS System packages installed via pacman : sudo pacman -S --needed python python-pip libpulse alsa-lib mesa libglvnd \\ libxcb xcb-util xcb-util-cursor xcb-util-image xcb-util-keysyms \\ xcb-util-renderutil xcb-util-wm libx11 libxkbcommon libxkbcommon-x11 \\ libxi libsm libice fontconfig freetype2 harfbuzz dbus libxrender \\ libxext libxrandr libxfixes libxcomposite libxdamage libxtst libxss \\ qt6-base qt6-multimedia Note On Arch, python3-venv is included in the python package. No separate install needed. Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS System packages installed via apt : sudo apt update sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip libpulse0 libasound2 \\ libegl1 libgl1 libopengl0 libglx-mesa0 libgl1-mesa-dri libegl1-mesa \\ libgles2 libxcb1 libxcb-cursor0 libxcb-icccm4 libxcb-image0 \\ libxcb-keysyms1 libxcb-randr0 libxcb-render0 libxcb-render-util0 \\ libxcb-shape0 libxcb-shm0 libxcb-sync1 libxcb-xfixes0 libxcb-xinerama0 \\ libxcb-xkb1 libxcb-util1 libx11-6 libx11-xcb1 libxkbcommon0 \\ libxkbcommon-x11-0 libxi6 libsm6 libice6 libfontconfig1 libfreetype6 \\ libharfbuzz0b libdbus-1-3 libxrender1 libxext6 libxrandr2 libxfixes3 \\ libxcomposite1 libxdamage1 libxtst6 libxss1 Fedora / RHEL / CentOS System packages installed via dnf : sudo dnf install -y python3 python3-pip pulseaudio-libs alsa-lib \\ mesa-libGL mesa-libEGL libglvnd-glx libxcb xcb-util xcb-util-cursor \\ xcb-util-image xcb-util-keysyms xcb-util-renderutil xcb-util-wm \\ libX11 libX11-xcb libxkbcommon libxkbcommon-x11 libXi libSM libICE \\ fontconfig freetype harfbuzz dbus-libs libXrender libXext libXrandr \\ libXfixes libXcomposite libXdamage libXtst libXScrnSaver qt6-qtbase \\ qt6-qtmultimedia Raspberry Pi Raspberry Pi OS (Debian-based) uses the same apt packages as Debian. The setup script automatically detects Raspberry Pi by reading /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model . OBD-II Bluetooth To use OBD-II via Bluetooth on Linux, add your user to the dialout group: sudo usermod -a -G dialout $USER Log out and back in for the change to take effect. Windows Installation Install Python 3.8+ from python.org . Check \"Add Python to PATH\" during installation. Clone and run setup : git clone https://github.com/waybettersolutions/octave.git cd octave python setup.py No system dependencies are needed on Windows. PySide6 bundles everything required. The venv activation command on Windows is venv\\Scripts\\activate . macOS Installation Install Python 3.8+ via Homebrew ( brew install python ) or from python.org . Clone and run setup : git clone https://github.com/waybettersolutions/octav"}, {"url": "hardware-managers.html", "title": "Hardware Managers", "subtitle": "ESP32 Volume Controller, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, and PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor", "headings": [], "body": "Hardware Managers - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Managers Hardware Managers ESP32 Volume Controller, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, and PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor ESP32 Volume Manager The ESP32VolumeManager in backend/esp32_volume_manager.py provides wireless rotary encoder volume control via a two-ESP32 setup communicating over ESP-NOW. An ESP32-S3 \"receiver dongle\" plugs into the computer via USB and forwards commands from a remote ESP32 encoder unit over serial. Linux Windows macOS ESP-NOW Wireless Protocol +-------------------+ ESP-NOW +-------------------+ | ESP32 Encoder | ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~> | ESP32-S3 Dongle | | (Rotary Knob) | (Wireless) | (USB Receiver) | | - Rotary encoder | | - USB Serial | | - Button press | | - LED ring | +-------------------+ +--------+----------+ | USB Serial 115200 baud | +--------v----------+ | OCTAVE | | ESP32VolumeManager| +-------------------+ ESP-NOW is Espressif's low-latency peer-to-peer wireless protocol. The encoder unit detects rotary knob rotation and button presses, then transmits simple text commands to the receiver dongle wirelessly. The dongle forwards these over USB serial to OCTAVE. Serial Communication The serial connection uses 115200 baud with a 1-second read/write timeout. A dedicated background thread ( _read_loop ) reads incoming data, buffering until complete newline-terminated lines are received: def _read_loop ( self ): buffer = \"\" while not self ._stop_thread and self ._serial_connection: if self ._serial_connection.in_waiting > 0 : data = self ._serial_connection.read( self ._serial_connection.in_waiting ) buffer += data.decode( 'utf-8' , errors= 'ignore' ) while '\\n' in buffer: line, buffer = buffer.split( '\\n' , 1 ) self ._process_command(line.strip()) else : time.sleep( 0.01 ) # Prevent CPU spinning Device Discovery The manager scans for serial ports using pyserial 's list_ports utility. ESP32-S3 devices are identified by Espressif's USB Vendor ID 0x303A or keywords like \"JTAG\" in the device description. Platform Detection Method Common Paths Windows COM port enumeration, VID:PID matching COM3 , COM4 , etc. macOS VID matching + glob patterns /dev/tty.usbmodem* , /dev/cu.usbserial* Linux VID matching + glob patterns /dev/ttyUSB* , /dev/ttyACM* The OBD-II Bluetooth port is excluded from results to prevent conflicts. Background scanning runs every 10 seconds when disconnected, and stops while connected. On startup (after a 3-second delay), the manager auto-selects the ESP32-S3 port if detected. If no ESP32-S3 is found, it falls back to the first available USB serial port. Command Protocol Incoming Commands (ESP32 to OCTAVE) Command Meaning Example + Volume up 1 tick Single clockwise rotation +N Volume up N ticks +3 = 3 ticks clockwise - Volume down 1 tick Single counter-clockwise rotation -N Volume down N ticks -2 = 2 ticks CCW M Mute toggle Encoder button press P Play/pause (reserved) Double-click (not implemented) Volume changes are multiplied by the configurable _step_size (default 1.0) and emitted as a float delta via volumeChangeRequested . This allows multi-tick accumulation for fast knob rotation. Outgoing Commands (OCTAVE to ESP32) Command Format Description Volume level V##\\n V75 = set LED ring to 75% Mute state M0\\n / M1\\n Sync mute indicator LED Theme color C###,###,###\\n C255,128,0 = set LED RGB color LED Color Sync The ESP32 receiver has an LED ring that displays volume level. OCTAVE sends: Volume updates -- Rate-limited to max 20 updates/sec (50ms debounce timer) to prevent LED flickering during rapid slider drags Theme colors -- send_theme_color(r, g, b) sends the current album art theme color to the LED ring Mute state -- send_mute_state(muted) syncs the mute indicator Keepalive System The ESP32 has a 5-second LED timeout. When LED sleep is disabled , OCTAVE sends keepalive volume updates every 2 seconds . When LED sleep is enabled, keepalives are stopped, allowing the LEDs to turn off after the timeout. Auto-Reconnect On connection loss (serial read error), the manager schedules automatic reconnection with exponential backoff: delay = min ( 10000 , 3000 * attempt_number) # 3s, 6s, 9s, 10s, 10s Maximum 5 reconnect attempts before giving up. The attempt counter resets on successful connection. Auto-reconnect can be disabled via settings. ESP32 Signals, Properties & Slots Signals Signal Parameters Description connectionStatusChanged str \"Connected\", \"Disconnected\", \"Connecting\", \"Error\" connectionDetailChanged str Human-readable status message availablePortsChanged list Detected serial ports updated volumeChangeRequested float Relative volume delta (can be fractional) muteToggleRequested -- Encoder button was pressed Properties Property Type Description connected bool Connection state connectionStatus str Status string connectionDetail str Detail message availablePorts list Available serial ports Slots Slot Description connect_device() Connect to the configured ESP32 port disconnect_device() Disconnect and stop all timers get_av"}, {"url": "hardware-setup.html", "title": "Hardware Setup", "subtitle": "Wiring, firmware, mounting, and configuration for all OCTAVE hardware peripherals.", "headings": [{"id": "esp32-volume", "text": "ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryimu", "text": "BerryIMU v3 IMU Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture-sensor", "text": "PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "3d-mounts", "text": "3D Printed Mounts", "level": 2}, {"id": "power", "text": "Power Supply Considerations", "level": 2}, {"id": "installation-tips", "text": "Vehicle Installation Tips", "level": 2}], "body": "Hardware Setup - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Setup Hardware Setup Wiring, firmware, mounting, and configuration for all OCTAVE hardware peripherals. ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob All Platforms The volume knob uses a two-ESP32 architecture with ESP-NOW for wireless communication. A remote encoder unit sends commands to a USB receiver dongle plugged into the computer running OCTAVE. Architecture [Rotary Encoder] ESP-NOW (2.4GHz) [USB Serial] [ + Button ] ----> [ESP32 Dev Board] ~~~~> [ESP32-S3 Dongle] ----> Computer [ + LED Ring ] \"Encoder Unit\" \"Receiver\" (OCTAVE) Sends: +, -, M Forwards to USB at 115200 baud Serial Protocol The ESP32-S3 receiver forwards simple text commands over USB serial at 115200 baud : Command Direction Description + or +N ESP32 -> OCTAVE Volume up (N ticks clockwise) - or -N ESP32 -> OCTAVE Volume down (N ticks counter-clockwise) M ESP32 -> OCTAVE Mute toggle (encoder button press) P ESP32 -> OCTAVE Play/pause (double-click, not yet implemented) V## OCTAVE -> ESP32 Current volume level (0-100) for LED display M0 / M1 OCTAVE -> ESP32 Mute state (0=unmuted, 1=muted) C###,###,### OCTAVE -> ESP32 Theme RGB color for LED ring (e.g., C255,128,0) ESP32-S3 Auto-Detection OCTAVE automatically detects ESP32-S3 USB devices by checking for the Espressif VID 303A in USB hardware IDs. Platform-specific port scanning: Windows: Scans COM ports, looks for VID 303A or \"USB JTAG/serial debug unit\" in description macOS: Checks /dev/tty.usbserial* , /dev/cu.usbmodem* , etc. Linux: Checks /dev/ttyUSB* , /dev/ttyACM* Auto-Connect: On startup, OCTAVE waits 3 seconds, scans for devices, and auto-selects the ESP32-S3 port if no port is configured. The OBD port is filtered out to avoid conflicts. Reconnection Behavior Max 5 reconnect attempts with exponential backoff (3s, 6s, 9s... max 10s) Background port scanning every 10 seconds when disconnected Keepalive timer sends volume updates every 2 seconds to prevent ESP32 LED timeout (5s) Volume updates rate-limited to 20/sec (50ms debounce) to prevent LED flickering LED Modes Theme mode: LED color follows album art extracted theme colors Static mode: LED uses a fixed user-chosen color Sleep mode: LEDs turn off after ESP32 timeout when OCTAVE stops sending keepalives BerryIMU v3 IMU Sensor Linux / Raspberry Pi The BerryIMU v3 provides 10 degrees of freedom (accelerometer, gyroscope, magnetometer, barometer) over I2C. OCTAVE uses Madgwick 9DOF AHRS for sensor fusion, outputting a quaternion directly to the QML 3D model. I2C Wiring BerryIMU v3 Raspberry Pi / SBC +-----------+ +-----------------+ | VCC (1) |----[ 3.3V ]---| Pin 1 (3.3V) | | GND (2) |----[ GND ]---| Pin 6 (GND) | | SDA (3) |----[ SDA ]---| Pin 3 (GPIO2) | I2C Bus 2 | SCL (4) |----[ SCL ]---| Pin 5 (GPIO3) | +-----------+ +-----------------+ I2C Chip Addresses Chip Address Function Init Rate LSM6DSL 0x6A Accelerometer + Gyroscope 416 Hz, +/-4g, 245 dps LIS3MDL 0x1C Magnetometer 155 Hz, +/-8 gauss BMP388 0x77 Barometer + Temperature Continuous mode with 21-byte calibration Madgwick AHRS Filter The Madgwick filter fuses all 9 axes into a single quaternion, avoiding gimbal lock entirely. Key parameters: Beta (gain): 0.03 (lower = smoother, higher = more responsive) Gyro deadband: 0.4 deg/s (zeros out noise when stationary) Sample interval: ~200 Hz (5ms read-loop period; sleeps out the remainder, never spins) Emit interval: ~60 Hz (16ms between signal emissions) Mounting Orientation Tare Calibration: Mount the BerryIMU in any orientation, then use the \"Calibrate Tare\" button in the sensor page to set the current position as zero reference. The tare offset is stored as a quaternion and applied to all subsequent readings. Enable I2C bus 2 first OCTAVE opens the BerryIMU and PAJ7620 on I2C bus 2 ( /dev/i2c-2 ), which is not exposed on the Raspberry Pi's GPIO header by default \u2014 pins 3/5 are i2c-1 out of the box. Expose bus 2 with a device-tree overlay in /boot/firmware/config.txt (e.g. an i2c-gpio overlay mapping SDA/SCL to your wired pins), reboot, and confirm ls /dev/i2c-2 succeeds before launching \u2014 otherwise the sensors fail with \u201cdevice not found.\u201d Startup Sequence 1 second delay after app start (QTimer.singleShot) Open I2C bus 2 via smbus2 Initialize LSM6DSL (accel 416Hz +/-4g, gyro 416Hz 245dps, BDU + auto-increment) Initialize LIS3MDL (ultra-high perf 155Hz, +/-8 gauss, continuous) Initialize BMP388 (read 21-byte calibration, enable continuous mode) Calibrate gyro bias (samples at rest) Start background read thread (daemon) Reconnection If initialization fails, OCTAVE retries up to 5 times with linear backoff (5s, 10s, 15s, 20s, 25s). Permission errors do not trigger retries (user must fix I2C permissions). PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor Linux / Raspberry Pi The PAJ7620U2 is a 9-gesture recognition sensor that detects hand motions for touchless control. It communicates over I2C and is polled at ~50Hz. I2C Wiring PAJ7620U2 Raspberry Pi / SBC +-----------+ +-----------------+ | VCC (1) |----[ 3.3V ]---| Pi"}, {"url": "index.html", "title": "OCTAVE Wiki", "subtitle": "The complete reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience.\n            Everything you need to understand, use, modify, and extend OCTAVE.", "headings": [], "body": "OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki The complete reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience. Everything you need to understand, use, modify, and extend OCTAVE. What is OCTAVE? OCTAVE is a fully programmable infotainment system designed for vehicles. It pairs a C++ / Qt 6 backend \u2014 the shipped binary on every platform \u2014 with a parallel Python / PySide6 backend for desktop development and tinkering, both driving a single shared QML frontend . It runs on Windows, macOS, Linux, Raspberry Pi, and Android. OCTAVE is purpose-built for older vehicles with outdated or missing infotainment systems, but works anywhere you want a rich media and diagnostics experience. The typical installation uses a Raspberry Pi (or equivalent SBC), a proportionate touchscreen, a power source, a 3D-printed mount, and a speaker interface. Development and testing can happen on any desktop OS. Core Features \u266b Local Media Player MP3, M4A, FLAC playback with album art carousel, shuffle/repeat, playlist management, and FFT waveform visualization. \u266c Spotify Integration Full Spotify Connect with OAuth2 authentication, device switching, playback control, and secure keyring token storage. >_ OBD-II Diagnostics 50+ vehicle parameters via ELM327 adapter. RPM, speed, coolant temp, fuel level, O2 sensors, DTC codes, and configurable gauge layouts. \u260f Phone Mirror & Android Auto Mirror your phone screen via scrcpy or run Android Auto through Google's Desktop Head Unit with touch forwarding. \u21a7 Music Download Search and download music with Spotify metadata matching and YouTube audio extraction. Auto-adds to your library. \u2699 Hardware Integrations ESP32 wireless volume knob (ESP-NOW), BerryIMU v3 9DOF sensor fusion (Madgwick AHRS), PAJ7620U2 gesture recognition. \u25c6 Dynamic Theming UI colors adapt in real-time to album art. Accent, background, and text colors extracted and applied across all views. \u223f Waveform Visualizer Real-time FFT waveform visualization with 96 frequency bars at 60 FPS, rendered directly in QML. Platform Support Platform Status Notes Linux (Debian/Arch/Fedora) Full support Primary development target. All features available. Raspberry Pi Full support Recommended for vehicle installation. All hardware I2C sensors supported. Windows 10+ Full support All features except I2C hardware (BerryIMU, gesture sensor). macOS 10.14+ Full support All features except I2C hardware. Android Experimental APK via Buildozer. Uses Android Bluetooth OBD and device sensors. Phone mirror/AA disabled. Wiki Sections Getting Started Installation & First Run \u2014 Setup on all platforms, requirements, configuration Architecture Overview \u2014 System design, communication patterns, threading model Backend Deep Dives Settings Manager \u2014 Central settings store, property system, JSON persistence Media Manager \u2014 Local audio playback, library scanning, album art Spotify Manager \u2014 OAuth2, API integration, device control OBD Manager \u2014 ELM327 protocol, 50+ vehicle parameters, threaded worker Network Manager \u2014 WiFi status, update checking, self-update Download Manager \u2014 Music search and download pipeline Hardware Managers \u2014 ESP32, BerryIMU, gesture sensor Phone Mirror & Android Auto \u2014 Scrcpy, DHU, frame providers Audio Analyzer \u2014 FFT waveform visualization Utilities \u2014 Clock, logging, platform detection, performance monitor Frontend Deep Dives Frontend Overview \u2014 QML architecture, Main.qml, page routing Pages & Views \u2014 Every page in the application Bottom Bar \u2014 Navigation and volume control Theme System \u2014 Dynamic theming, design tokens Settings UI \u2014 Settings framework, layout engines, components Components \u2014 Reusable QML components Reference & Guides Settings Reference \u2014 Complete catalog of every setting Signals & Slots API \u2014 Every manager's QML-accessible API Hardware Setup \u2014 Wiring, firmware, 3D printed mounts Building & CI/CD \u2014 PyInstaller, platform builds, GitHub Actions Development \u2014 Dev mode, mock data, MCP server, profiling Troubleshooting \u2014 Common issues, log locations, debugging Tech Stack Component Technology Purpose Backend Python 3.8+ All business logic, hardware integration, API calls Frontend QML (Qt Quick 2.15) Declarative UI with animations and 3D effects Qt Binding PySide6 6.5+ Python-Qt bridge with signals/slots Audio QMediaPlayer + av Playback and FFT analysis Spotify spotipy Spotify Web API wrapper with OAuth2 OBD-II python-obd ELM327 adapter communication Music DL yt-dlp + ytmusicapi YouTube audio extraction with Spotify metadata Sensors smbus2 I2C communication (BerryIMU, gesture) Serial pyserial ESP32 volume knob USB communication Credentials keyring Secure OS keychain storage for Spotify tokens Build PyInstaller / Buildozer Standalone executables and Android APK Quick Links Installation Guide Architecture Settings Reference Build Guide Developer Guide Troubleshooting"}, {"url": "media-manager.html", "title": "Media Manager", "subtitle": "Local media playback engine with QMediaPlayer, album art extraction, color theming, and playlist management", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-formats", "text": "Supported Audio Formats", "level": 2}, {"id": "qmediaplayer", "text": "QMediaPlayer Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "library-scanning", "text": "Library Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "metadata-caching", "text": "Metadata Caching", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art", "text": "Album Art Extraction", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-extraction", "text": "Color Extraction and Theming", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-control", "text": "Playback Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle-repeat", "text": "Shuffle Logic", "level": 2}, {"id": "playlist-management", "text": "Playlist Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume-mute", "text": "Volume and Mute", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback State Resume", "level": 2}, {"id": "precaching", "text": "Neighbor Pre-caching", "level": 2}, {"id": "error-recovery", "text": "Error Recovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Key Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Key Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "extending", "text": "How to Modify / Extend", "level": 2}], "body": "Media Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Media Manager Media Manager Local media playback engine with QMediaPlayer, album art extraction, color theming, and playlist management Overview The MediaManager class in backend/media_manager.py handles all local audio file playback for OCTAVE. It wraps PySide6's QMediaPlayer and QAudioOutput , manages a metadata and album art cache, extracts dominant colors from album art for dynamic theming, and provides full playlist support with shuffle, skip, and playback resume. It is registered as a QML context property named mediaManager and exposes all playback controls as Slots, with state changes broadcast via Signals. All Platforms Supported Audio Formats The following file extensions are recognized during library scanning: AUDIO_EXTENSIONS = ( '.mp3' , '.m4a' , '.flac' , '.ogg' , '.opus' , '.wav' ) MP3 files use the mutagen library's ID3 and MP3 classes for metadata. M4A/MP4/AAC files use mutagen.mp4.MP4 . Other formats fall back to mutagen.File for basic duration information. QMediaPlayer Integration MediaManager creates a QMediaPlayer with a QAudioOutput in its constructor. Key configuration: self._player = QMediaPlayer() self._audio_output = QAudioOutput() self._player.setAudioOutput(self._audio_output) self._audio_output.setVolume( 0.5 ) # Increase probe size for reliable format detection self._player.setProperty( \"probe-size\" , 10000000 ) # 10MB self._player.setProperty( \"analyzeduration\" , 5000000 ) # 5 seconds Connected signals from QMediaPlayer: durationChanged - forwarded directly to QML positionChanged - forwarded directly to QML mediaStatusChanged - routed to _handle_media_status() for end-of-track and error detection errorOccurred - triggers automatic playback recovery A QTimer at 100ms interval supplements position updates for smooth UI slider tracking. Library Scanning The media directory defaults to backend/media/ but is typically overridden by the user via SettingsManager's mediaFolder property (defaults to ~/Music on desktop, /sdcard/Music on Android). get_media_files() scans the configured directory for files matching AUDIO_EXTENSIONS : @Slot(result= list ) def get_media_files (self, emit_signal= True ): mp3_files = [] if os.path.exists(self.media_dir): for file in os.listdir(self.media_dir): if file.lower().endswith(AUDIO_EXTENSIONS): mp3_files.append(file) if emit_signal: self.mediaListChanged.emit(mp3_files) return mp3_files The scanProgress signal provides terminal-style feedback during deeper scans (e.g., when building the \"All Music\" playlist across subdirectories). Path Safety The helper is_safe_path(base_path, target_path) validates that any resolved path stays within the base directory, preventing path traversal attacks from malicious filenames. Metadata Caching Metadata is cached in self._metadata_cache (dict: filename to metadata dict) with a maximum of 1000 entries. Each entry stores: { \"artist\" : \"Artist Name\" , \"album\" : \"Album Name\" , \"title\" : \"Track Title\" , \"duration\" : 245 # seconds } The _cache_metadata() method handles format-specific extraction: MP3 : Uses ID3() for tags (TPE1, TALB, TIT2) and MP3() for duration. Also extracts album art from APIC frames in the same read. M4A/MP4/AAC : Uses mutagen.mp4.MP4 . Tags are \\xa9ART , \\xa9alb , \\xa9nam . Album art from covr tags. Others (FLAC, OGG, etc.) : Falls back to mutagen.File for duration only. All metadata strings are sanitized by sanitize_metadata() which: Removes control characters Collapses multiple spaces Truncates to 200 characters with ellipsis Album Art Extraction Album art is cached in self._album_art_cache (dict: album_id to file:// URL) with a maximum of 500 cached files on disk in the backend/temp/ directory. Album ID Generation Each album is identified by a composite key: \"{album}_{artist}\" . If metadata is unavailable, a SHA-256 hash of the filename is used as fallback. Extraction Flow Check if album_id is already in _album_art_cache (instant return) Run LRU cache management (evict least-accessed item if at capacity) Read album art from the audio file: MP3: iterate ID3 frames for APIC tags M4A: read covr tags Write image data to temp file with SHA-256 hash-based filename (e.g., cover_a1b2c3d4.jpg ) Store the file:// URL in the cache and return it Optimization: Single-Read Cache Population The _cache_metadata() method also calls _cache_album_art_from_id3() to extract album art from the already-loaded ID3 object. This means a single file read populates both the metadata cache and the album art cache, making subsequent get_album_art() calls instant. LRU Cache Management def _manage_cache (self, new_album_id): if len(self._album_art_cache) >= self._max_cache_files: # Find least recently accessed item items = [(k, v) for k, v in self._access_count.items() if k != new_album_id] least_used = min(items, key= lambda x: x[ 1 ])[ 0 ] # Remove file from disk and cache ... Color Extraction and Theming When the \"Album Art Capture\" theme is active, MediaManager extracts dominant col"}, {"url": "network-manager.html", "title": "Network Manager", "subtitle": "WiFi connectivity detection, GitHub-based update checking, and self-update capability", "headings": [], "body": "Network Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Network Manager Network Manager WiFi connectivity detection, GitHub-based update checking, and self-update capability Overview The NetworkManager is responsible for three core functions within OCTAVE: detecting network connectivity status, checking for application updates via the GitHub API, and performing git-based self-updates when running from source. It is defined in backend/network_manager.py and registered as a QML context property in main.py . Thread Safety All blocking I/O operations (subprocess calls, HTTP requests) run on a background ThreadPoolExecutor with a single worker. Results are polled back to the main Qt thread via a QTimer at 250ms intervals, ensuring signals are always emitted on the main thread. The manager targets the GitHub repository WayBetterSolutions/OCTAVE and compares the local git HEAD against the remote main branch to determine update availability. Architecture NetworkManager | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (1 worker) | |-- _do_network_check() Network name + internet check | |-- _do_update_check_sync() GitHub API comparison | |-- _do_self_update_sync() git fetch + reset + verify | |-- QTimer (_poll_timer) 30s network polling |-- QTimer (_result_poll_timer) 250ms result/progress polling | |-- Signals --> QML networkNameChanged(str) isConnectedChanged(bool) updateStatusChanged(str) selfUpdateStatusChanged(str) Background Work Pattern Only one background job runs at a time. The _submit_work() method accepts a callable and a result type tag, submits to the executor, and starts the result poll timer: def _submit_work ( self , fn, result_type): \"\"\"Submit blocking work to the thread pool. Only one job at a time.\"\"\" if self ._pending_future and not self ._pending_future.done(): return # Already busy self ._pending_future = self ._executor.submit(fn) self ._pending_future._result_type = result_type self ._result_poll_timer.start() When the poll timer fires, _check_pending_result() checks if the future is done, extracts the result, and routes it to the appropriate handler based on the _result_type tag: \"network_status\" , \"update_check\" , or \"self_update\" . WiFi Connectivity Detection Network status is polled every 30 seconds via _poll_timer , with an initial check 2 seconds after startup to let the UI load first. Each check runs two operations on the worker thread: Network Name Detection The static method _get_network_name_sync() uses platform-specific commands to determine the active connection name: Platform Method Command Linux NetworkManager CLI nmcli -t -f ACTIVE,SSID dev wifi macOS networksetup networksetup -getairportnetwork en0 Windows netsh netsh wlan show interfaces Android dumpsys dumpsys wifi (rooted devices) Each platform also has a fallback check for wired Ethernet connections. All subprocess calls use a 5-second timeout. Internet Connectivity Check The _check_internet_sync() method makes a lightweight HTTP request to Google's 204-response endpoint: @staticmethod def _check_internet_sync (): try : urllib.request.urlopen( \"https://clients3.google.com/generate_204\" , timeout= 5 ) return True except Exception: pass return False Auto Update Check on First Connect When connectivity is first detected ( _auto_update_checked flag), the manager automatically triggers checkForUpdates() with a 500ms delay. This ensures users are notified of available updates as soon as the system comes online. Update Checking Mechanism The update checker compares the local git commit hash against the latest commit on the main branch of the GitHub repository. Step-by-Step Flow Get local commit hash -- Runs git rev-parse --short HEAD from the repository root directory to get the 7-character short hash. Query GitHub API -- Fetches https://api.github.com/repos/WayBetterSolutions/OCTAVE/commits/main with a User-Agent: OCTAVE-Updater header. Classify the relationship -- Truncates the remote SHA to 7 characters. A bare inequality test cannot distinguish \"behind\" from \"ahead\", so the check also asks git how the two commits relate: if the remote commit is already an ancestor of local HEAD , the checkout is ahead (a dev build), not out of date. If the remote commit is unknown locally but local commits exist that the last-fetched origin/main lacks, the branch has diverged . Emit result -- Sets updateStatus to one of: \"up-to-date\" , \"update-available\" , \"ahead\" , \"diverged\" , or \"error\" . Also populates the remote commit hash and message, and refreshes workingTreeDirty . Update Status Values # updateStatus can be one of: \"checking\" # Currently checking \"up-to-date\" # Local matches remote \"update-available\" # Local is strictly behind the remote \"ahead\" # Source checkout with local commits (dev build) \"diverged\" # Both sides moved -- not a safe update \"error\" # Check failed (no internet, API error) Only \"update-available\" raises the update badge and offers the self-update button. \"ahead\" and \"diverged\" are normal states when running from the source tree and must never trigger a"}, {"url": "obd-manager.html", "title": "OBD Manager", "subtitle": "OBD-II vehicle diagnostics with threaded connections, 80+ parameter support, and an Android Bluetooth variant", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "architecture", "text": "Architecture and Files", "level": 2}, {"id": "connection-flow", "text": "Connection Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "threaded-worker", "text": "Threaded Connection Worker", "level": 2}, {"id": "speed-optimizations", "text": "Speed Optimizations", "level": 2}, {"id": "device-discovery", "text": "Device Discovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "auto-reconnect", "text": "Auto-Reconnect", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-watchdog", "text": "Data Watchdog", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-parameters", "text": "Supported OBD Parameters", "level": 2}, {"id": "watcher-system", "text": "Watcher System", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-scanning", "text": "Vehicle Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnostics", "text": "Diagnostics (DTC)", "level": 2}, {"id": "elm327-protocol", "text": "elm327_protocol.py", "level": 2}, {"id": "android-variant", "text": "AndroidOBDManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Key Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Key Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "unified-adapter-api", "text": "Unified Adapter API", "level": 2}], "body": "OBD Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / OBD Manager OBD Manager OBD-II vehicle diagnostics with threaded connections, 80+ parameter support, and an Android Bluetooth variant Overview The OBDManager class in backend/obd_manager.py provides real-time vehicle diagnostics via the OBD-II protocol. It connects to an ELM327-compatible adapter (USB, Bluetooth, or WiFi), monitors over 80 engine parameters, and supports diagnostic trouble code (DTC) reading and clearing. On Android, the AndroidOBDManager in backend/android_obd_manager.py provides the same signal interface using Classic Bluetooth RFCOMM sockets (with BLE as fallback) and the pure-Python ELM327 protocol handler in backend/elm327_protocol.py . All Platforms Architecture and Files File Platform Description backend/obd_manager.py Desktop Main OBD manager using the python-obd library with async connection backend/elm327_protocol.py All Pure-Python ELM327 protocol: PID table, response parsing, DTC decoding backend/android_obd_manager.py Android Android variant using Qt Bluetooth RFCOMM sockets Desktop: Android: +------------------+ +------------------+ | OBDManager | | AndroidOBDManager| | (python-obd) | | (Qt Bluetooth) | +--------+---------+ +--------+---------+ | | obd.Async() QBluetoothSocket (serial/BT) (RFCOMM / BLE) | | +--------+---------+ +--------+---------+ | ELM327 Adapter | | ELM327 Adapter | +--------+---------+ +--------+---------+ | | OBD-II Port OBD-II Port (Vehicle) (Vehicle) Connection Flow The connection process is entirely non-blocking, using a QThread worker pattern: Startup : A single-shot 50ms timer ( _startup_timer ) defers the initial connection from the constructor Device scan : _scan_for_devices() checks for available serial ports (platform-specific patterns) Port check : _check_port_exists() verifies the configured port is present Worker creation : An OBDConnectionWorker is created and moved to a QThread Blocking connection : The worker calls obd.Async() on its thread Result handling : Signals deliver the result back to the main thread: CAR_CONNECTED : Sets up watchers, starts data polling, begins connection monitor ELM_CONNECTED : Adapter found but no vehicle; schedules retry Failed: Emits error status, schedules auto-reconnect Auto-scan : 2 seconds after successful connection, scan_vehicle() runs to detect supported parameters Threaded Connection Worker The OBDConnectionWorker class runs the blocking obd.Async() call in a separate QThread to avoid freezing the UI: class OBDConnectionWorker (QObject): connectionComplete = Signal( object , object ) # (connection, status) connectionProgress = Signal( int , str ) # (progress, message) connectionError = Signal( str ) def do_connect (self): connection = obd.Async( portstr=self._port, fast=self._fast_mode, timeout=self._timeout, delay_cmds= 0 , # MAXIMUM SPEED protocol=self._cached_protocol ) status = connection.status() self.connectionComplete.emit(connection, status) The worker is wired up via Qt's thread affinity pattern: self._worker = OBDConnectionWorker() self._worker.set_params(port, fast_mode, timeout, cached_protocol) self._worker_thread = QThread() self._worker.moveToThread(self._worker_thread) self._worker.connectionProgress.connect(self._on_connection_progress) self._worker.connectionComplete.connect(self._on_connection_complete) self._worker.connectionError.connect(self._on_connection_error) self._worker_thread.started.connect(self._worker.do_connect) self._worker_thread.start() Speed Optimizations OCTAVE applies several optimizations for faster OBD communication: Optimization Setting Effect Zero command delay delay_cmds=0 No delay between polling cycles (default python-obd uses 0.25s) Cached protocol protocol=self._cached_protocol Skips auto-detection on reconnect (saves 1-3 seconds) Reduced timeout timeout=5 5 second connection timeout (down from default 10s) Fast mode fast=True Uses python-obd fast mode for optimized polling Force watch force=True Bypasses supported_commands check when setting up watchers Protocol Caching After a successful connection, the protocol name is cached in _last_successful_protocol . On reconnect, this is passed to obd.Async() to skip the slow auto-detection phase. The cache is cleared on I/O errors (errno 5) since the rfcomm link may have dropped. Device Discovery The _scan_for_devices() method runs on a 10-second timer and checks platform-specific paths: Platform Scanned Paths Windows All COM ports via serial.tools.list_ports (filters for Bluetooth/OBD/ELM/USB keywords) macOS /dev/tty.OBD* , /dev/tty.Bluetooth* , /dev/cu.OBD* , /dev/tty.usbserial* , /dev/tty.*ELM* Linux /dev/rfcomm* , /dev/ttyUSB* , /dev/ttyACM* (ttyS* excluded as false positives) When a new port matching the configured port appears, an automatic connection attempt is triggered. The availablePortsChanged signal notifies QML of discovered ports. Auto-Reconnect Auto-reconnect is configurable via SettingsManager's obdAutoReconnectAttempts (0 = disabled, 1-10 = max attempts). "}, {"url": "pages.html", "title": "Pages", "subtitle": "Every top-level view in the OCTAVE StackView: what it shows, which managers it uses, and notable QML techniques", "headings": [{"id": "mainmenu", "text": "MainMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaroom", "text": "MediaRoom.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaplayer", "text": "MediaPlayer.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdmenu", "text": "OBDMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obddiagnostics", "text": "OBDDiagnostics.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "carmenu", "text": "CarMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensormenu", "text": "SensorMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "clockmenu", "text": "ClockMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "phonemirror", "text": "PhoneMirrorView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "androidauto", "text": "AndroidAutoView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloadpage", "text": "DownloadPage.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation-summary", "text": "Page Navigation Summary", "level": 2}], "body": "Pages - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Pages Pages Every top-level view in the OCTAVE StackView: what it shows, which managers it uses, and notable QML techniques MainMenu.qml frontend/MainMenu.qml is the home screen and the initialItem of the StackView. It presents a two-column dashboard with media controls on the left and OBD telemetry on the right. Layout RowLayout { anchors.fill : parent spacing : App.Spacing. dp ( 10 ) // LEFT: Media section (33% width) Rectangle { Layout.preferredWidth: parent.width * 0.33 } // RIGHT: OBD section (fills remaining 67%) Rectangle { Layout.fillWidth: true } } Media Section Features Blurred album art background \u2014 Uses GaussianBlur on the current album art with a dark overlay for readability Album art image with DropShadow effect Auto-scrolling text \u2014 Song title and artist/album use a Flickable with a Timer -driven NumberAnimation that scrolls horizontally when text overflows Seek slider \u2014 Custom-styled Slider with accent-colored fill and a circular handle Dual media source \u2014 The useSpotify flag determines whether properties come from mediaManager or spotifyManager OBD Section Embeds the HomeOBDView component which displays a configurable subset of OBD parameters in a compact stacked layout. Clicking either section navigates to the relevant full page. Backend Managers Used mediaManager , spotifyManager , settingsManager , obdManager MediaRoom.qml frontend/MediaRoom.qml is the full-screen \"now playing\" view with large album art, playback controls, and optional waveform visualizer. Key Features AlbumArtCarousel \u2014 The centerpiece uses the reusable AlbumArtCarousel component with configurable transitions (Crossfade, Slide, Vinyl Lift, Dissolve, Flip, and 3D modes like Coverflow, Conveyor, Stack) Blurred background \u2014 Full-screen album art blur behind all content, with configurable blur radius Scrolling metadata \u2014 Song title, artist, and album with auto-scroll for long text Seek bar with position/duration display Transport controls \u2014 Previous, Play/Pause, Next, Shuffle buttons with press animations Empty playlist detection \u2014 When no local songs exist, shows a download button instead of controls Cached DownloadPage navigation \u2014 The openDownloadPage() function reuses a cached page instance stored on the StackView Spotify Integration The useSpotify property switches all track info, playback commands, and progress between local and Spotify sources. Spotify track info is cached in dedicated properties updated via Connections signals. Side Card Preloading MediaRoom pre-fetches album art for adjacent tracks to ensure instant coverflow transitions: // Hidden preloaders decode next/prev art at full resolution Image { id : prevArtPreloader visible : false ; width : 1 ; height : 1 sourceSize : Qt. size (albumArtStack.artSize * 2 , ...) asynchronous : true ; cache : true } Image { id : nextArtPreloader visible : false ; width : 1 ; height : 1 sourceSize : Qt. size (albumArtStack.artSize * 2 , ...) asynchronous : true ; cache : true } A sideCardRefreshTimer fires on the next event-loop tick after track changes, using a batched backend call ( get_neighbor_album_arts() ) to minimize PySide6 bridge crossings. Animation and Performance Track change animations are carefully sequenced to avoid frame stuttering: Kill the visualizer instantly (snap bars to zero before animation) Trigger the carousel slide animation Defer FFT analysis until after the settled signal fires Fade the visualizer back in after a brief delay function animateTrackChange () { vizFadeInTimer. stop () _cardAnimBusy = true waveformContainer._vizFadedOut = true waveformContainer. dropBars () albumArtStack. triggerSlide () } Backend Managers Used mediaManager , spotifyManager , settingsManager , audioAnalyzer , downloadManager MediaPlayer.qml frontend/MediaPlayer.qml is the playlist/library browser view showing all songs in a scrollable list. Key Features Playlist management \u2014 Local and Spotify playlists are listed and switchable. Properties track both playlistNames and spotifyPlaylistNames Song list \u2014 Each row shows index, album art thumbnail (via LazyImage), title, artist, and album Playback state highlighting \u2014 Currently playing song is highlighted with playingHighlightColor ; paused songs use pausedHighlightColor Initial scroll position \u2014 On page load, the list scrolls to the currently playing song Cached DownloadPage \u2014 Uses the same caching pattern as MediaRoom, stored on stackView._cachedDownloadPage Force model refresh \u2014 A playlistRefreshCounter is incremented when switching between local and Spotify playlists to force delegate rebinding Backend Managers Used mediaManager , spotifyManager , settingsManager , downloadManager OBDMenu.qml frontend/OBDMenu.qml displays live OBD-II vehicle data in a responsive grid of parameter cards. Layout GridLayout { id : parametersGrid columns : 3 // Adapts: 2 for \u22644 params, 3 for \u22649, 4 for more rowSpacing : App.Spacing. dp ( 10 ) columnSpacing : App.Spacing. dp ( 10 ) Repeater { model : allPara"}, {"url": "phone-mirror-android-auto.html", "title": "Phone Mirror &amp; Android Auto", "subtitle": "scrcpy-based phone mirroring and Google DHU-based Android Auto integration", "headings": [], "body": "Phone Mirror & Android Auto - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Phone Mirror & Android Auto Phone Mirror & Android Auto scrcpy-based phone mirroring and Google DHU-based Android Auto integration Overview OCTAVE supports two methods of integrating a connected Android phone into the infotainment display: Phone Mirror -- Mirrors the entire phone screen using scrcpy , providing full access to all phone apps Android Auto -- Runs Google's Desktop Head Unit (DHU) to provide the standard Android Auto car experience Both systems use the same architectural pattern: launch an external process, capture its window content, and display it within OCTAVE's QML interface via custom QQuickImageProvider subclasses. This avoids file I/O for frame transfer. Windows Platform Limitation Window capture and embedding currently use Windows-specific APIs (GDI, PrintWindow, SetParent). The capture classes check platform.system() == \"Windows\" and emit errors on other platforms. Linux/macOS support would require alternative capture backends. +------------------+ USB/ADB +------------------+ | Android Phone | | OCTAVE | +------------------+ | | | +-----------+ | scrcpy | Frame | | (PrintWindow) | | Provider | | | +-----------+ | | | | | QML Image | | (scrcpyframe:/) | | (dhuframe:/) | +------------------+ Phone Mirror The phone mirror subsystem consists of four files in backend/phone_mirror/ : File Class Purpose manager.py PhoneMirrorManager Process lifecycle, ADB device management, scrcpy discovery scrcpy_capture.py ScrcpyCapture , ScrcpyFrameProvider , ScrcpyCaptureItem Window capture, frame streaming, touch input embedded_widget.py EmbeddedScrcpyItem QQuickItem for direct window embedding via SetParent scrcpy_container.py Container widget Layout container for the embedded widget PhoneMirrorManager The PhoneMirrorManager in backend/phone_mirror/manager.py is the central orchestrator. It owns the singleton scrcpy process (only one instance allowed at a time) and handles executable discovery, device detection, and process lifecycle. Signals Signal Parameters Description scrcpyPathChanged -- Effective scrcpy path changed error str Error message scrcpyStarted int scrcpy is ready: native window handle (Windows) or process id (Linux v4l2 mode) scrcpyStopped -- scrcpy process ended scrcpyError str scrcpy-specific error (too-old version, device unauthorized/offline/absent, scrcpy's own ERROR lines on exit) isRunningChanged -- isRunning changed videoDeviceChanged -- v4l2 loopback node changed displaySizeChanged -- Requested virtual display size changed displayIdChanged -- Android display id / active display size of the running mirror changed Properties Property Type Description isScrcpyInstalled bool Whether scrcpy is found scrcpyPath str Path to scrcpy executable adbPath str Path to ADB executable isRunning bool Whether scrcpy process is active scrcpyWindowHandle int Native window handle of scrcpy (Windows) or its pid (Linux) scrcpyVersion str Version of the effective scrcpy binary, e.g. \"3.3.4\" ; \"\" if unknown. Versions below 2.0 are rejected at startScrcpy() captureMode str \"window\" (Windows: screen-grab), \"v4l2\" (Linux: headless scrcpy into a v4l2loopback node rendered by QtMultimedia), \"unsupported\" (macOS, mobile) videoDevice str v4l2loopback node used in v4l2 mode (default /dev/video10 , setting scrcpyVideoDevice ) displaySize str Requested virtual display \"WxH\" passed as --new-display (default 1280x800 , setting scrcpyDisplaySize ); \"\" mirrors the phone's own screen. Dimensions are snapped down to multiples of 8 displayId int Android display id of the running mirror, -1 for the phone's main display. Touch injection targets it via adb shell input -d ID activeDisplaySize str \"WxH\" of the virtual display actually in use, \"\" when mirroring the phone screen Slots Slot Returns Description setScrcpyPath(str) , setAudioEnabled(bool) , setVideoDevice(str) , setDisplaySize(str) -- Configuration; restart scrcpy if it is running hasConnectedDevice() bool An authorized device is attached getDeviceState() str \"device\" / \"unauthorized\" / \"offline\" / \"none\" / \"no-adb\" -- lets the UI say why mirroring cannot start getDeviceSerial() , getDeviceName() , getDeviceResolution() , getDeviceSdk() str / int ADB probes videoDeviceExists() bool The loopback node exists on disk (module loaded) startScrcpy() , stopScrcpy() , cleanup() -- Process lifecycle getInstallInstructions() str Platform-specific install text scrcpy & ADB Discovery Both executables are discovered using a priority-based search: Bundled location -- Check tools/scrcpy/ relative to the project root (or PyInstaller _MEIPASS ) System PATH -- shutil.which(\"scrcpy\") / shutil.which(\"adb\") Common install locations -- Platform-specific paths (e.g., C:\\scrcpy\\ , /usr/bin/scrcpy , Android SDK paths) Every candidate (bundled, PATH, common locations) is validated by running scrcpy --version ; the parsed version is exposed as scrcpyVersion . A candidate that does not run is skipped. A version below 2.0 is still found"}, {"url": "settings-manager.html", "title": "Settings Manager", "subtitle": "Central settings store with JSON persistence, Qt Property system, and dynamic registry for UI rendering", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "file-location", "text": "File Location and Storage Paths", "level": 2}, {"id": "property-signal-system", "text": "Property / Signal / Slot System", "level": 2}, {"id": "json-persistence", "text": "JSON Persistence", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-registry", "text": "Settings Registry Metadata System", "level": 2}, {"id": "default-settings", "text": "Default Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "key-methods", "text": "Key Methods and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "section-visibility", "text": "Section Visibility System", "level": 2}, {"id": "pinned-settings", "text": "Pinned Quick Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "generic-access", "text": "Generic Setting Access", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-colors", "text": "Album Art Colors", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-connection", "text": "Connection to QML", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-new-setting", "text": "How to Add a New Setting", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Manager Settings Manager Central settings store with JSON persistence, Qt Property system, and dynamic registry for UI rendering Overview The SettingsManager class in backend/settings_manager.py is the central configuration store for all of OCTAVE. It inherits from QObject and exposes every user-configurable option to QML via Qt's Property , Signal , and Slot decorators. All state is persisted to a single JSON file ( settingsConfigure.json ) in an OS-specific data directory. In addition to individual typed properties, SettingsManager provides a SETTINGS_REGISTRY metadata system that maps setting IDs to control types, labels, categories, and save slots. This registry powers the dynamic Quick Panel UI, allowing settings to be rendered as sliders, toggles, or chip selectors without any QML-side per-setting code. All Platforms File Location and Storage Paths The settings file is stored at an OS-specific location determined by get_app_data_dir() : Platform Path Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE\\settingsConfigure.json macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Linux ~/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE/ ) Android <cwd>/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json (app-private internal storage) The get_app_data_dir() function creates the directory if it does not exist: def get_app_data_dir (): from backend.platform_config import IS_ANDROID app_name = \"OCTAVE\" if IS_ANDROID: data_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), app_name) elif sys.platform == 'win32' : base = os.environ.get( 'APPDATA' , os.path.expanduser( '~' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) elif sys.platform == 'darwin' : data_dir = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), 'Library' , 'Application Support' , app_name) else : base = os.environ.get( 'XDG_CONFIG_HOME' , os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), '.config' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) os.makedirs(data_dir, exist_ok= True ) return data_dir Property / Signal / Slot System Every setting follows a consistent three-part pattern using PySide6 decorators: Signal declaration at the class level (e.g., themeSettingChanged = Signal(str) ) Property decorator that exposes the value to QML with a notify signal Slot method that QML calls to save the value, updates internal state, persists to JSON, and emits the signal Here is a representative example for the themeSetting property: # 1. Signal declaration (class level) themeSettingChanged = Signal( str ) # 2. Property with notify signal @Property( str , notify=themeSettingChanged) def themeSetting (self): return self._theme_setting # 3. Slot for QML to call @Slot( str ) def save_theme_setting (self, theme): logger.debug(f \"Saving theme setting: {theme}\" ) self._theme_setting = theme self.update_setting( \"themeSetting\" , theme, self.themeSettingChanged) The update_setting() helper loads the current JSON, updates the key, saves atomically, and optionally emits the signal: def update_setting (self, key, value, signal= None ): settings = self.load_settings() settings[key] = value self.save_settings(settings) if signal: signal.emit(value) Property Types Used Settings use str , bool , int , float , and 'QVariantList' as Property types. Boolean properties must be checked before int in validation because bool is a subclass of int in Python. JSON Persistence Settings are stored in settingsConfigure.json and saved atomically with cross-platform file locking. Atomic Save Strategy The save_settings() method uses a write-to-temp-then-rename approach: Validate the settings dict against _default_settings Write to a temporary file in the same directory via tempfile.mkstemp() Acquire an exclusive file lock (platform-specific: fcntl on Unix, msvcrt on Windows) Write JSON with indent=4 Set restrictive file permissions ( chmod 600 on Unix) Atomic rename to the final path (on Windows, removes old file first since os.rename cannot overwrite) On any failure, falls back to direct write Validation The _validate_settings() method ensures type correctness by comparing each loaded value against the type of its default. Booleans are explicitly checked before int fields. Extra keys not in defaults are preserved for forward compatibility. File Locking def _lock_file (self, f, exclusive= True ): if sys.platform == 'win32' : msvcrt.locking(f.fileno(), msvcrt.LK_NBLCK, 1 ) else : fcntl.flock(f.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB) Settings Registry Metadata System The SETTINGS_REGISTRY is a module-level dictionary that maps setting IDs to metadata objects. This allows the QML Quick Panel to dynamically render controls without per-setting UI code. Registry Entry Structure Field Type Description key str The camelCase key in settingsConfigure.json and the QML property name label str Human-readable label displayed in the UI category str Settings section (e.g., \"mediaSettings\" , \"displaySettings\" ) controlType str UI control: \"toggle\" , \"slider\" , or \"chips\" saveSlot str Name of the Python @Slot meth"}, {"url": "settings-reference.html", "title": "Settings Reference", "subtitle": "Complete reference for every setting in OCTAVE, with types, defaults, valid ranges, and the settings page where each appears.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "device", "text": "Device Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "media", "text": "Media Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32", "text": "ESP32 Volume Knob Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "imu", "text": "IMU Sensor Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture", "text": "Gesture Sensor Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "android-auto", "text": "Android Auto Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-mirror", "text": "Phone Mirror Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback Resume Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "download", "text": "Download Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "internal", "text": "Internal / UI State Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "persistence", "text": "Persistence &amp; File Format", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Reference Settings Reference Complete reference for every setting in OCTAVE, with types, defaults, valid ranges, and the settings page where each appears. Overview All settings are managed by backend/settings_manager.py and persisted to settingsConfigure.json in the OS-specific config directory: Windows: %APPDATA%/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Linux: ~/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Android: <cwd>/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Settings are loaded at startup, validated against the default schema, and saved atomically (write to temp file, then rename). File locking is used cross-platform to prevent corruption from concurrent access. Quick Panel: Many settings are registered in the SETTINGS_REGISTRY dictionary, which powers the dynamic Quick Panel UI. These settings can be pinned by users for quick access. Device Settings Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values Description deviceName deviceName str \"Default Device\" Any string User-assigned name for this OCTAVE installation fontSetting fontSetting str \"Google Sans\" Any font name UI font family used throughout the application windowState windowState str \"windowed\" \"windowed\", \"fullscreen\", \"maximized\" Application window mode on startup screenWidth screenWidth int 1280 Any positive int Window width in pixels screenHeight screenHeight int 720 Any positive int Window height in pixels Media Settings These settings appear on the Media Settings page and control the media player, album art, and audio visualization. Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values Description startUpVolume startUpVolume float 0.1 0.0 - 1.0 (step 0.01, logarithmic) Volume level when app starts. Applied via log curve: (v)^2.0 autoPlayOnStartup autoPlayOnStartup bool false true/false Auto-play last song on app launch persistShuffleState persistShuffleState bool false true/false Remember shuffle on/off between sessions showWaveformVisualizer showWaveformVisualizer bool true true/false Show FFT waveform visualization in MediaRoom (96 bars at 60 FPS) show3DButtonTilt show3DButtonTilt bool false true/false 3D tilt animation on prev/play/next button press show3DAlbumPreview show3DAlbumPreview bool false true/false Coverflow-style album art card stack display roundedAlbumArt roundedAlbumArt bool true true/false Rounded corners on album art images albumArtCornerRadius albumArtCornerRadius int 16 2 - 32 (step 1) Corner radius in dp when rounded album art is enabled showAlbumArtShadow showAlbumArtShadow bool true true/false Drop shadow behind album art vinylRecordMode vinylRecordMode bool false true/false Spin album art like a vinyl record during playback albumArtTransition albumArtTransition str \"Crossfade\" \"Crossfade\", \"Slide\", \"Vinyl Lift\", \"Dissolve\", \"Flip\", \"Coverflow\", \"Conveyor\", \"Stack\", \"Depth\", \"Swing\" Track-change animation. Single setting regardless of 3D preview \u2014 when 3D preview is on, side cards animate along; when off, they're hidden and only the center card animates. backgroundOverlayOpacity backgroundOverlayOpacity int 80 0 - 100 (step 1) Dark overlay opacity percentage over blurred album art background backgroundBlurRadius backgroundBlurRadius int 40 0 - 100 (step 1) Gaussian blur radius for album art background sideCardOpacity sideCardOpacity float 0.4 0.1 - 1.0 (step 0.05) Opacity of 3D preview side album cards sideCardAngle sideCardAngle int 30 5 - 60 (step 1) Rotation angle of 3D preview side cards in degrees buttonTiltDuration buttonTiltDuration int 200 50 - 500 (step 10) Button tilt animation duration in milliseconds textScrollSpeed textScrollSpeed int 5000 1000 - 10000 (step 500) Marquee text scroll animation duration in milliseconds mediaFolder mediaFolder str \"~/Music\" Any valid path Root music library directory mediaSource mediaSource str \"local\" \"local\", \"spotify\" Active media source for playback songLengthTransition songLengthTransition bool false true/false Animate theme color transition over song duration musicButtonDefaultPage musicButtonDefaultPage str \"mediaRoom\" \"mediaRoom\", \"mediaPlayer\" Which page the music nav button opens by default returnToLibraryAfterSelection returnToLibraryAfterSelection bool false true/false Return to MediaPlayer view after selecting a song in library Display Settings Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values Description uiScale uiScale float 1.0 0.5 - 2.0 (step 0.05) Global UI scaling factor themeSetting themeSetting str \"CosmicVoyager\" Any theme name (built-in or custom) Active color theme name showClock showClock bool true true/false Show clock display in the UI clockFormat24Hour clockFormat24Hour bool true true/false 24-hour clock format (false = 12-hour AM/PM) clockSize clockSize int 18 8 - 72 Clock font size in points colorTransitionMs colorTransitionMs int 1000 0 - 5000 (step 100) Theme color transition animation duration in ms showBottomBarMediaControls showBottomBarMediaControls bool true tru"}, {"url": "settings-ui.html", "title": "Settings UI", "subtitle": "Settings page routing, four layout engines, reusable components, the widget system, and how to add new settings pages", "headings": [{"id": "orchestrator", "text": "SettingsMenu Orchestrator", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-model", "text": "Page Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "layouts", "text": "Layout Engines", "level": 2}, {"id": "carousel", "text": "Carousel Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "sidebar", "text": "Sidebar Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "hub", "text": "Hub Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboard", "text": "Dashboard Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "components", "text": "Reusable Components", "level": 2}, {"id": "widgets", "text": "Widget System", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-page", "text": "Adding a New Settings Page", "level": 2}, {"id": "layout-switching", "text": "Layout Switching", "level": 2}, {"id": "visibility", "text": "Section Visibility", "level": 2}, {"id": "expanded-cards", "text": "Card State Persistence (ExpandedCards.js)", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings UI - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings UI Settings UI Settings page routing, four layout engines, reusable components, the widget system, and how to add new settings pages SettingsMenu Orchestrator frontend/SettingsMenu.qml is the orchestrator that manages settings navigation. It does not render settings content itself; instead it delegates to one of four layout engines based on user preference. Item { id : settingsMenu objectName : \"settingsMenu\" required property var stackView required property var mainWindow required property string initialSection property string layoutStyle: settingsManager ? settingsManager.settingsLayoutStyle : \"Carousel\" } The layout style determines which component is loaded. The orchestrator provides the hubModel (filtered list of visible pages) and helper functions to each layout. Page Model The pageModel is the single source of truth for all settings pages: readonly property var pageModel: [ { name : \"Display\" , section : \"displaySettings\" , source : \"settings/DisplaySettingsPage.qml\" , widget : \"widgets/DisplayWidget.qml\" , icon : \"\\u263C\" , iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_display.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Layout\" , \"Window\" , \"Appearance\" , \"Clock\" ] }, { name : \"Media\" , section : \"mediaSettings\" , source : \"settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml\" , widget : \"widgets/MediaWidget.qml\" , icon : \"\\u266B\" , iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"media_button.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Library\" , \"Playback\" , \"Now Playing\" , \"Spotify\" ] }, // ... 5 more pages ] Each entry defines: Key Purpose name Display name shown in tabs/sidebar section Internal identifier used for navigation and persistence source QML file path (relative to frontend/ ) widget Widget QML file for Dashboard layout (relative to frontend/settings/ ) icon Legacy Unicode character; kept only as a fallback iconSource Theme-tinted SVG in frontend/assets/ , built from Style.assetBase . This is what actually renders. group Category grouping: Appearance, Connectivity, System subSections Named anchors within the page for sub-navigation. Must match the page's tileModel titles exactly \u2014 lookup goes through findChildByObjectName() , and pages set each card's objectName from modelData.title . Settings Pages Page File Covers Display settings/DisplaySettingsPage.qml Theme, environment, layout, window size, clock, UI scale, fonts Media settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml Library paths, playback, album art transitions, blur, visualizer, Spotify OBD settings/OBDSettingsPage.qml Connection (status + live log), Adapters (scan/port/manual entry), Tuning (fast mode, retries, gauge style), Parameters (PID visibility) Accessories settings/AccessoriesSettingsPage.qml Volume Knob, IMU sensor, gesture sensor, Phone Dock (Android Auto + Phone Mirror) Device settings/DeviceSettingsPage.qml Device Name, Network, Power About settings/AboutPage.qml Version info, credits, licenses Layout Engines OCTAVE provides four distinct settings layout styles, all rendering the same pages with different navigation paradigms. Carousel Layout frontend/SettingsCarouselLayout.qml presents settings pages as a horizontal SwipeView with a tab strip at the top. Tab Strip Row { id : tabStrip anchors.horizontalCenter : parent.horizontalCenter // Tabs divide available width equally property real tabWidth: hubModel.length > 0 ? (parent.width - spacing * (hubModel.length - 1 )) / hubModel.length : App.Spacing. dp ( 80 ) Repeater { model : hubModel.length Rectangle { color : isCurrent ? Qt. rgba (App.Style.accent.r, ..., 0.2 ) : \"transparent\" } } } Each tab shows an icon + name, with accent-colored highlight for the active tab and an orange dot for update notifications. The SwipeView below allows horizontal swiping between pages or tab clicking. Sidebar Layout frontend/SettingsSidebarLayout.qml is a traditional two-panel layout with a sidebar navigation list on the left and content on the right. RowLayout { spacing : 0 Rectangle { // Left sidebar panel Layout.preferredWidth: App.Spacing.settingsNavWidth color : App.Style.sidebarColor } Loader { // Right content area Layout.fillWidth: true source : sourceForSection (settingsMenu.currentSection) } } Visual Enhancements Subtle overhead lighting gradient on the sidebar Lit edge highlight along the top Soft drop shadow on the right edge Scroll memory (via ScrollMemory.js ) preserves scroll position when switching pages Hub Layout frontend/SettingsHubLayout.qml presents a card grid landing page. Clicking a card opens its detail page full-width. Two-State Navigation property string viewState: \"\" // \"hub\" or \"detail\" The hub state shows a grid of SettingsHubGridCard components, each displaying a summary widget with live info lines (theme name, connection status, etc.). The detail state loads the full settings page in a Loader . Widget Info Lines function getWidgetForSection (section) { switch (section) { case \"displaySettings\" : return [ { label : \"Theme\" , value : settingsManager.themeSetting }"}, {"url": "signals-slots-reference.html", "title": "Signals &amp; Slots Reference", "subtitle": "Complete API reference for every Signal, Slot, and Property exposed to QML by backend managers.", "headings": [{"id": "media-manager", "text": "MediaManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "spotify-manager", "text": "SpotifyManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-manager", "text": "OBDManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-manager", "text": "SettingsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32-volume", "text": "ESP32VolumeManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryimu", "text": "BerryIMUManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture", "text": "GestureManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "audio-analyzer", "text": "AudioAnalyzer", "level": 2}, {"id": "perf-monitor", "text": "PerfMonitor", "level": 2}], "body": "Signals & Slots Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Signals & Slots Reference Signals & Slots Reference Complete API reference for every Signal, Slot, and Property exposed to QML by backend managers. Context Properties: All managers are registered in main.py via engine.rootContext().setContextProperty() . QML accesses them by name: mediaManager.next_track() , settingsManager.themeSetting , etc. MediaManager backend/media_manager.py \u2014 QML name: mediaManager Local MP3/M4A/FLAC/OGG/OPUS/WAV playback via QMediaPlayer. Manages playlists, metadata, album art extraction, and color theming. Signals Signal Parameters Fires When playbackStateChanged int (QMediaPlayer state) Player state changes (playing/paused/stopped) playStateChanged bool (isPlaying) Play/pause state toggles currentMediaChanged str (filename) Current track changes mediaListChanged list (filenames) Media library file list updates muteChanged bool (isMuted) Mute state toggles durationChanged int (ms) Track duration becomes known positionChanged int (ms) Playback position updates (every 100ms) metadataChanged str, str, str (title, artist, album) ID3 metadata loaded for current track durationFormatChanged str (formatted) Duration formatted as \"M:SS\" changes volumeChanged float (0.0-1.0) Volume level changes shuffleStateChanged bool Shuffle on/off toggles totalDurationChanged str (formatted) Library total duration recalculated albumCountChanged int Unique album count changes artistCountChanged int Unique artist count changes playlistsChanged (none) Playlist list updates (subfolder scan) currentPlaylistChanged str (name) Active playlist changes scanProgress str (message) Terminal-style feedback during library scan albumColorsExtracted str (JSON) Album art colors extracted for theming Key Slots Slot Parameters Returns Description play() - - Start/resume playback pause() - - Pause playback toggle_play() - - Toggle play/pause next_track() - - Skip to next track previous_track() - - Skip to previous track toggle_mute() - - Toggle mute (guard prevents rapid toggles) toggle_shuffle() - - Toggle shuffle mode setVolume(float) float (0.0-1.0) - Set audio output volume set_position(int) int (ms) - Seek to position set_media_file(str) str (filename) - Play a specific file by name get_current_file() - str Get current playing filename get_display_name(str) str (filename) str Get cleaned display name for a file get_album_art(str) str (filename) str (URL) Get album art URL (cached, extracted from ID3) get_band(str) str (filename) str Get artist name from metadata get_album(str) str (filename) str Get album name from metadata load_media_files() - - Scan media folder and load files is_playing() - bool Check if currently playing is_muted() - bool Check mute state is_shuffled() - bool Check shuffle state SpotifyManager backend/spotify_manager.py \u2014 QML name: spotifyManager Spotify Connect API integration via spotipy. Controls playback on connected devices. Credentials stored in OS keychain via keyring . Signals Signal Parameters Fires When connectionStateChanged bool Authenticated/disconnected from Spotify errorOccurred str API error or auth failure playStateChanged bool Play/pause state on Spotify device currentTrackChanged str, str, str, str (title, artist, album, art_url) Track changes on Spotify durationChanged int (ms) Track duration available positionChanged int (ms) Position updated (interpolated at 250ms locally, polled at 3s from API) shuffleStateChanged bool Shuffle toggled on Spotify volumeChanged int (0-100) Volume changed on Spotify device devicesChanged list Available Spotify Connect devices update activeDeviceChanged str Active playback device changes playlistsChanged list User playlists loaded from Spotify spotifyTracksChanged list Tracks from selected playlist loaded authUrlReady str (URL) OAuth URL ready for user browser auth statusProgress str Terminal-style status messages during auth/load albumColorsExtracted str (JSON) Album art colors extracted from Spotify cover queueUpdated (none) Queue neighbor art data ready for side cards Key Slots Slot Parameters Returns Description start_auth() - - Begin OAuth flow (opens browser) toggle_play() - - Toggle play/pause on Spotify next_track() - - Skip to next track previous_track() - - Skip to previous track set_volume(int) int (0-100) - Set Spotify volume is_connected() - bool Check if authenticated disconnect() - - Disconnect and clear tokens OBDManager backend/obd_manager.py \u2014 QML name: obdManager OBD-II vehicle diagnostics via python-obd. Uses a dedicated QThread worker for non-blocking connection. Supports 87+ PIDs, DTC reading/clearing, and vehicle scanning. Sensor Signals (87+ total) Each emits float when the sensor value updates. Original 18 core signals: Signal OBD PID Unit rpmChanged RPM rev/min speedMPHChanged SPEED mph coolantTempChanged COOLANT_TEMP deg F voltageChanged CONTROL_MODULE_VOLTAGE V engineLoadChanged ENGINE_LOAD % throttlePositionChanged THROTTLE_POS % intakeAirTempChanged INTAKE_TEMP de"}, {"url": "spotify-manager.html", "title": "Spotify Manager", "subtitle": "Spotify Connect integration with OAuth2 authentication, secure token storage, and async API calls", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "credential-setup", "text": "Credential Setup", "level": 2}, {"id": "oauth-flow", "text": "OAuth2 Authentication Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "keyring-storage", "text": "Keyring Token Storage", "level": 2}, {"id": "async-pattern", "text": "Async API Call Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-control", "text": "Playback Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "position-interpolation", "text": "Position Interpolation", "level": 2}, {"id": "device-management", "text": "Device Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "playlist-management", "text": "Playlist Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "queue-and-sidecards", "text": "Queue and Side Cards", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-theming", "text": "Album Art Theming", "level": 2}, {"id": "disconnect-cleanup", "text": "Disconnect and Cleanup", "level": 2}, {"id": "error-handling", "text": "Error Handling", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Key Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Key Slots", "level": 2}], "body": "Spotify Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Spotify Manager Spotify Manager Spotify Connect integration with OAuth2 authentication, secure token storage, and async API calls Overview The SpotifyManager class in backend/spotify_manager.py controls Spotify playback on connected devices (phone, desktop, smart speaker, etc.) via the Spotify Web API. It mirrors the MediaManager signal interface so QML can treat both local and Spotify playback through a unified UI. SpotifyManager does not play audio directly -- it acts as a remote control for Spotify Connect devices, sending play/pause/skip/seek/volume commands over the API and polling for playback state updates every 3 seconds. Desktop Dependencies Package Purpose Required? spotipy Spotify Web API wrapper and OAuth2 management Yes (graceful degradation if missing) keyring Secure OS-level token storage (Keychain, Credential Manager) Optional (falls back to file cache) Both libraries are checked at import time with feature flags: try : import spotipy from spotipy.oauth2 import SpotifyOAuth SPOTIPY_AVAILABLE = True except ImportError: SPOTIPY_AVAILABLE = False try : import keyring KEYRING_AVAILABLE = True except ImportError: KEYRING_AVAILABLE = False Credential Setup Users must provide a Spotify Developer application's Client ID and Client Secret. These are stored in settingsConfigure.json via SettingsManager: @Slot( str , str ) def set_credentials (self, client_id, client_secret): self._client_id = client_id self._client_secret = client_secret if self._settings_manager: self._settings_manager.save_spotify_credentials(client_id, client_secret) Credentials are loaded from SettingsManager when connect_settings_manager() is called during app initialization. Security Note Client ID and Secret are stored in the settings JSON file, not in the OS keychain. Only the OAuth tokens are stored securely via keyring. The redirect URI is hardcoded to http://127.0.0.1:8888/callback . OAuth2 Authentication Flow The authenticate() method implements the full OAuth2 Authorization Code flow with CSRF protection: +------------------+ +------------------+ +------------------+ | OCTAVE QML | | SpotifyManager | | Spotify API | | | | | | | | authenticate() --|---->| Check cached | | | | | | token via | | | | | | KeyringCache | | | | | | | | | | | | If cached: | | | | | | Create client --|---->| Validate token | | | | Start polling | | | | | | | | | | | | If no token: | | | | | | Generate state | | | | authUrlReady | Return tokens | | | | _authCompleted | | | | | | (main thread) | | | +------------------+ +------------------+ +------------------+ Required OAuth Scopes scope = ( \"user-read-playback-state \" \"user-modify-playback-state \" \"user-read-currently-playing \" \"playlist-read-private \" \"playlist-read-collaborative \" \"user-library-read\" ) CSRF Protection A cryptographically random state parameter is generated via secrets.token_urlsafe(32) and stored in self._oauth_state . The callback handler validates this state before accepting the authorization code: received_state = params.get( 'state' , [ None ])[ 0 ] if received_state != manager._oauth_state: # Return 403 - possible CSRF attack manager.errorOccurred.emit( \"OAuth state mismatch\" ) return Local Callback Server A one-shot HTTP server runs on 127.0.0.1:8888 in a daemon thread. It handles a single request (the OAuth callback) with a 2-minute timeout, then shuts down. The server class is defined inline as CallbackHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler) . Thread-Safe Post-Auth Setup After the callback receives the token, the auth_manager is stored in _pending_auth_manager and the internal _authCompleted signal is emitted. This signal is connected to _on_auth_completed() which runs on the main thread and sets up the Spotipy client, starts polling timers, and refreshes devices and playlists. Keyring Token Storage The KeyringCacheHandler class implements Spotipy's CacheHandler interface to store OAuth tokens securely in the OS keychain: Platform Storage Backend Windows Windows Credential Manager macOS macOS Keychain Linux Secret Service (GNOME Keyring / KWallet) class KeyringCacheHandler (_CacheHandlerBase): SERVICE_NAME = \"OCTAVE_Spotify\" USERNAME = \"spotify_token\" def get_cached_token (self): if KEYRING_AVAILABLE: token_string = keyring.get_password(self.SERVICE_NAME, self.USERNAME) if token_string: return json.loads(token_string) # Fallback to file cache if os.path.exists(self._cache_file): with open(self._cache_file, 'r' ) as f: return json.load(f) return None If keyring is unavailable, tokens fall back to a file-based cache at backend/.spotify_token_cache . Key methods: get_cached_token() - Read from keyring, fall back to file save_token_to_cache(token_info) - Write to keyring, fall back to file delete_cached_token() - Remove from both keyring and file Async API Call Pattern All Spotify API calls run asynchronously via a ThreadPoolExecutor with a maximum of 2 concurrent workers (to avoid rate limiting): self._executor = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_wo"}, {"url": "theme-system.html", "title": "Theme System", "subtitle": "Dynamic theming with Style.qml, environment presets via EnvironmentTheme.qml, and responsive layout scaling from Spacing.qml", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "style-qml", "text": "Style.qml &mdash; Color Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "builtin-themes", "text": "Built-in Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "custom-themes", "text": "Custom Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-tokens", "text": "Color Token Reference", "level": 2}, {"id": "transitions", "text": "Color Transitions", "level": 2}, {"id": "environment-theme", "text": "EnvironmentTheme.qml &mdash; UI Shape Presets", "level": 2}, {"id": "env-presets", "text": "Environment Presets", "level": 2}, {"id": "spacing-qml", "text": "Spacing.qml &mdash; Layout Scaling", "level": 2}, {"id": "scaling-functions", "text": "Scaling Functions", "level": 2}, {"id": "customization", "text": "Customization Guide", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-system", "text": "Font System", "level": 2}, {"id": "consuming-themes", "text": "How Components Consume the Theme", "level": 2}], "body": "Theme System - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Theme System Theme System Dynamic theming with Style.qml, environment presets via EnvironmentTheme.qml, and responsive layout scaling from Spacing.qml Overview OCTAVE's theming is powered by three pragma Singleton QML objects that collectively control every visual aspect of the UI: +------------------+ +---------------------+ +------------------+ | Style.qml | | EnvironmentTheme.qml| | Spacing.qml | | | | | | | | COLORS | | SHAPES | | SIZES | | - base, accent | | - card radius | | - dp(), dpMin() | | - text, sliders | | - button style | | - scaledSize() | | - obd, bottombar | | - slider ticks | | - scalePx() | | - transitions | | - corner brackets | | - percentage | | - album art | | - scanline effects | | properties | +------------------+ +---------------------+ +------------------+ | | | +------------------------+-------------------------+ | All accessed via App.* namespace Style.qml \u2014 Color Themes frontend/Style.qml is the central color theme store. It defines built-in themes, supports custom themes loaded from settings, and provides the Album Art Capture feature. Theme Resolution The activeTheme property drives all color bindings. It resolves in priority order: property var activeTheme: { // 1. Album Art Capture (dynamic, real-time) if (currentTheme === \"Album Art Capture\" && albumArtTheme) return albumArtTheme // 2. Custom themes (loaded from settings) if (customThemes[currentTheme]) return customThemes[currentTheme] // 3. Built-in themes return themes[currentTheme] || themes[ \"SolarizedLight\" ] } Built-in Themes OCTAVE ships with 9 built-in themes. Each is a JavaScript object with a standardized structure: Theme Base Accent Character SolarizedLight #FDF6E3 #268BD2 Warm cream with blue accents NeonMatrix #0C0C0C #00FF41 Black with green terminal glow CosmicVoyager #0A0E17 #00BFFF Deep space with cyan highlights TechnoRetro #1E1014 #FF00AA Dark with hot pink neon AutumnCascade #2D1E12 #E67E22 Warm brown with orange VividGradient #12001A #C837AB Deep purple with magenta DesertOasis #201007 #D2B48C Dark earth with tan/sage QuantumNebula #0F0F1A #9C27B0 Dark indigo with purple/cyan Album Art Capture #1a1a2e #e94560 Dynamic (placeholder colors shown) Theme Object Structure { \"base\" : \"#0C0C0C\" , // Primary background \"baseAlt\" : \"#161616\" , // Secondary background \"accent\" : \"#00FF41\" , // Primary accent color \"text\" : { \"primary\" : \"#CCFFCC\" , // Main text \"secondary\" : \"#88CC88\" // Subdued text }, \"states\" : { \"hover\" : \"#202020\" , // Hover background \"paused\" : \"#1C1C1C\" , // Paused track highlight \"playing\" : \"#282828\" // Playing track highlight }, \"sliders\" : { \"volume\" : \"#00FF41\" , // Volume slider fill \"media\" : \"#88CC88\" , // Media room slider \"settings\" : \"#00FF41\" // Settings sliders }, \"bottombar\" : { \"previous\" : \"#00FF41\" , // Each button has its own color \"play\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"pause\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"next\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"volume\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"shuffle\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"toggleShade\" : \"#202020\" , \"homeButton\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"obdButton\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"mediaButton\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"settingsButton\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"androidAutoButton\" : \"#00FF41\" , \"phoneMirrorButton\" : \"#00FF41\" }, \"mediaroom\" : { ... }, // MediaRoom button colors \"mainmenu\" : { \"mediaContainer\" : \"#005500\" }, \"obd\" : { \"boxBackground\" : \"#161616\" , \"barColor\" : \"#33FF66\" } } Custom Themes Users can create custom themes that are stored in settingsConfigure.json . They are loaded at startup in Main.qml : let customThemes = settingsManager.customThemes customThemes. forEach ( function (themeName) { let themeJSON = settingsManager. get_custom_theme (themeName) let themeObj = JSON. parse (themeJSON) App.Style. addCustomTheme (themeName, themeObj) }) When custom themes change at runtime (via the customThemesChanged signal), the entire custom themes map is rebuilt atomically to avoid briefly clearing the active theme. Album Art Capture The \"Album Art Capture\" theme extracts dominant colors from the current song's album art and generates a full theme object dynamically. The pipeline: A new track starts playing in mediaManager or spotifyManager Python extracts dominant colors from the album art image A complete theme JSON is generated and emitted via albumColorsExtracted(themeJson) signal Main.qml receives the signal and calls Style.updateAlbumArtTheme(themeJson) Style.qml parses the JSON, stores it as albumArtTheme , forces activeTheme to re-evaluate by toggling currentTheme All color properties with Behavior on animations smoothly transition to the new palette Color Token Reference Style.qml exposes these reactive color properties, all with Behavior on { ColorAnimation { duration: colorTransitionMs } } : Background Colors Property Source backgroundColor activeTheme.base headerBackgroundColor activeTheme.baseAlt sidebarColor activeTheme.base contentColor activeTheme.baseAlt bottomBarGradientStart activeTheme.base bottomBarGradientEnd activeTheme.baseAlt obdBoxBackground activeTheme.obd.boxBackground Text "}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "subtitle": "Common issues, platform-specific fixes, log file analysis, and debugging techniques.", "headings": [{"id": "installation", "text": "Installation Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display &amp; Rendering Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "audio", "text": "Audio Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD Connection Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "spotify", "text": "Spotify Authentication Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "hardware-sensors", "text": "Hardware Sensor Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "logs", "text": "Log Files &amp; Debug Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "android", "text": "Android-Specific Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "wsl", "text": "WSL Display Issues", "level": 2}], "body": "Troubleshooting - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Troubleshooting Troubleshooting Common issues, platform-specific fixes, log file analysis, and debugging techniques. Installation Issues Windows Problem Solution \"DLL not found\" errors Install Visual C++ Redistributable PySide6 fails to install Ensure Python 3.8+ (64-bit). Run: pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel then retry pip install obd fails Install build tools: Visual Studio Build Tools with \"Desktop development with C++\" Virtual environment won't activate Run Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser in PowerShell macOS Problem Solution \"App is damaged\" Gatekeeper error xattr -cr /Applications/OCTAVE.app or sudo spctl --master-disable PySide6 import errors on ARM Mac Ensure using native ARM Python, not Rosetta x86. Check with python -c \"import platform; print(platform.machine())\" keyring fails Grant terminal access to Keychain in System Settings > Privacy Linux Problem Solution Missing libxcb libraries sudo apt install libxcb-xinerama0 libxcb-cursor0 libxkbcommon0 libGL errors sudo apt install libgl1-mesa-glx libegl1 smbus2 install fails sudo apt install python3-dev i2c-tools AppImage won't run chmod +x OCTAVE-x86_64.AppImage or install libfuse2: sudo apt install libfuse2 AppImage FUSE not available Extract: ./OCTAVE-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract && ./squashfs-root/AppRun Display & Rendering Issues XCB Plugin Errors (Linux) Error: qt.qpa.plugin: Could not load the Qt platform plugin \"xcb\" Install required X11 libraries: sudo apt install libxcb-xinerama0 libxcb-cursor0 libxkbcommon-x11-0 \\ libxcb-icccm4 libxcb-image0 libxcb-keysyms1 libxcb-randr0 \\ libxcb-render-util0 libxcb-shape0 Wayland vs X11 OCTAVE uses Qt Quick which works on both Wayland and X11. If you experience issues on Wayland: # Force X11 backend export QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb python main.py # Or force Wayland export QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland python main.py OpenGL / Software Rendering If the UI is black or has rendering artifacts: # Force software rendering (slow but reliable) export QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software python main.py # Or specify OpenGL ES (for Raspberry Pi) export QT_OPENGL=es2 python main.py Black Screen on Startup Check QML import path: OCTAVE adds frontend/ to the QML import path. If running from a different directory, QML files won't be found. Check Qt Quick Controls style: OCTAVE forces QT_QUICK_CONTROLS_STYLE=Basic . If this env var is overridden, slider customization breaks. Run with --debug and check logs for QML errors. UI Scaling Issues If the UI is too small or too large, adjust the uiScale setting (0.5 - 2.0) in Settings > Display, or set it in settingsConfigure.json . Audio Issues No Audio Output Check volume: OCTAVE uses a logarithmic curve (volume/100)^2.0 . Low slider values produce very quiet output. Check mute state: mediaManager.is_muted() in QML or check the mute toggle guard ( _mute_toggle_locked ). Verify audio output device in system settings. PulseAudio Issues (Linux) # Check PulseAudio is running pulseaudio --check # Restart PulseAudio pulseaudio --kill && pulseaudio --start # List audio sinks pactl list short sinks ALSA Errors (Linux) # Install ALSA plugins sudo apt install libasound2-plugins # If \"ALSA lib pcm_dmix.c\" errors appear, set: export QT_MULTIMEDIA_PREFERRED_PLUGINS=gstreamer Audio Playback Stalls The media manager has automatic recovery for stalled/invalid media. If a file consistently fails, check the file format. Supported: MP3, M4A, FLAC, OGG, OPUS, WAV. OBD Connection Issues Serial Port Permissions (Linux) # Add user to dialout group for serial port access sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER # Log out and back in, then verify groups | grep dialout Bluetooth OBD Pairing (Linux) # Pair the OBD adapter bluetoothctl scan on pair XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX trust XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX quit # Bind to rfcomm sudo rfcomm bind 0 XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX # Verify ls -la /dev/rfcomm0 Connection Timeouts Default timeout is 5 seconds. The OBD manager uses a dedicated QThread worker to avoid blocking the UI. If connection is slow, check if obdFastMode is enabled (it should be by default). The manager caches the last successful protocol to skip auto-detection on reconnect (saves 1-3 seconds). Set obdAutoReconnectAttempts to 1-10 for automatic reconnection after drops. \"No Data\" from Vehicle Run a vehicle scan (OBD Settings > Scan Vehicle) to discover which PIDs your vehicle supports. Not all vehicles support all 87+ parameters. Enable only supported ones to avoid polling errors. Some parameters only report data when the engine is running. Data Watchdog The OBD manager includes a data watchdog that detects stale connections (no data for 30 seconds while parameters are watched). If triggered, it automatically reconnects. Spotify Authentication Issues OAuth Flow Enter Client ID and Client Secret in Spotify Settings Click \"Connect\" - OCTAVE starts a local HTTP server on port 8888 Browser opens to Spotify's auth page After authorizing, Spotify redirects to http://127.0.0.1:888"}, {"url": "utilities.html", "title": "Utility Modules", "subtitle": "Reference documentation for Clock, Logging, Platform Config, Performance Monitor, Command Server, and Stubs.", "headings": [{"id": "clock", "text": "Clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging Configuration", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-config", "text": "Platform Config", "level": 2}, {"id": "perf-monitor", "text": "Performance Monitor", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command Server", "level": 2}, {"id": "stubs", "text": "Android Stubs", "level": 2}], "body": "Utility Modules - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Utility Modules Utility Modules Reference documentation for Clock, Logging, Platform Config, Performance Monitor, Command Server, and Stubs. Clock backend/clock.py \u2014 QML name: clock A simple QTimer-based clock that emits the formatted time string every second. Respects the user's clock visibility and format preferences from SettingsManager. Source Code class Clock (QObject): timeChanged = Signal(str) def __init__ (self, settings_manager): super ().__init__() self._settings_manager = settings_manager self.timer = QTimer() self.timer.timeout.connect(self.update_time) self.timer.start( 1000 ) # Update every second def update_time (self): if not self._settings_manager.showClock: self.timeChanged.emit( \"\" ) return current_time = datetime.now() if self._settings_manager.clockFormat24Hour: time_str = current_time.strftime( \"%H:%M\" ) else : hour_min = current_time.strftime( \"%I:%M\" ) am_pm = current_time.strftime( \"%p\" ).upper() time_str = f\"{hour_min} {am_pm}\" self.timeChanged.emit(time_str) Behavior Timer interval: 1000ms (1 second) Format: Reads clockFormat24Hour setting each tick. 24h: \"14:30\", 12h: \"02:30 PM\" Visibility: When showClock is false, emits empty string so QML hides the display QML binding: clock.timeChanged signal carries the formatted time string Logging Configuration backend/logging_config.py Centralized logging for the entire application. Provides two main functions: setup_logging(debug, console, file) Called once at startup in main.py . Initializes the Python logging system with rotating file handlers. from backend.logging_config import setup_logging setup_logging(debug= False ) # Normal mode setup_logging(debug= True ) # Debug mode (--debug flag) Parameters Parameter Type Default Description debug bool False Enable DEBUG level logging. Creates octave-debug.log (10MB, 2 backups). console bool True Enable console (stdout) output file bool True Enable file output with automatic rotation Log Rotation Policy File Level Max Size Backups Total Max octave.log INFO+ 5 MB 3 ~20 MB octave-error.log ERROR+ 2 MB 5 ~12 MB octave-debug.log DEBUG+ 10 MB 2 ~30 MB Total max disk usage: ~62 MB with debug, ~32 MB without. Designed for embedded/vehicle use where disk space is limited. Formatters # Console (compact): HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | module_name | message # File (detailed): YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | name | file.py:line | func() | message get_logger(name) Gets a hierarchical logger for a module. Converts Python module paths to clean octave.* names. from backend.logging_config import get_logger logger = get_logger(__name__) logger.info( \"Application started\" ) Name Mapping Module __name__ Logger Name backend.obd_manager octave.obd backend.media_manager octave.media backend.spotify_manager octave.spotify backend.settings_manager octave.settings backend.clock octave.clock backend.android_auto.manager octave.android_auto backend.phone_mirror.manager octave.phone_mirror backend.phone_mirror.scrcpy_host octave.phone_mirror.host __main__ octave.app Other backend.* octave.<rest> Implementation Notes Logger instances are cached in a module-level _loggers dict for fast repeat access _initialized flag prevents double-initialization Log directory is created automatically at the platform-specific config path + /logs/ Platform Config backend/platform_config.py Single source of truth for Android platform detection. All platform-conditional code should import from here rather than doing its own detection. Source Code import sys import os IS_ANDROID = hasattr (sys, 'getandroidapilevel' ) or 'ANDROID_ROOT' in os.environ Functions Function Returns Description IS_ANDROID bool (constant) True if running on Android (checks sys.getandroidapilevel or ANDROID_ROOT env var) get_android_data_dir() str Returns <cwd>/OCTAVE/ , creates directory if needed get_android_media_dir() str Returns /sdcard/Music/ , creates directory if needed Usage Pattern from backend.platform_config import IS_ANDROID if IS_ANDROID: # Use stub managers, Android paths, etc. from backend.stubs import StubOBDManager as OBDManager else : from backend.obd_manager import OBDManager Used by: settings_manager.py (config directory), logging_config.py (log directory), main.py (manager selection), media_manager.py (default music folder). Performance Monitor backend/perf_monitor.py Comprehensive performance monitoring exposed as a QObject with QML-bindable properties. Only active when --profile flag is passed. Internal Components _RingBuffer Fixed-size ring buffer (deque-based) for numeric samples with cheap percentile calculation. Used for both event-loop latency and GIL jitter measurements. Capacity: 300 samples for GIL, 600 for loop (about 10 seconds at 60Hz). _GILProbe A daemon background thread that sleeps for 10ms intervals and measures the actual sleep duration. The jitter (actual - expected) approximates GIL contention time. Runs continuously until stopped. Event-Loop Probe A QTimer with interval 0 (fires on next event-loop"}];
